        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "The denial-then-fix-batch pattern (task 282 denial \u00e2\u2020\u2019 task 284 recon \u00e2\u2020\u2019 task 285 developer \u00e2\u2020\u2019 task 286 ratification) demonstrated that a well-specified denial rationale with enumerated sub-findings can serve directly as the specification for a subsequent fix round. Each of the four task-282 sub-findings (C1 cross-reference, C3 ecm:source, C5 imported-terms scope, CANONICAL_RESERVED_NAME_COLLISION omission) mapped one-to-one to a fix change, with no ambiguity about scope. The pattern is efficient when the denial rationale is written with fix-round consumption in mind: named findings with bounded scope, not holistic rejection."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "A schema-boundary discipline applies when the same property name (ecm:source) appears in both term-object and instance-object contexts: validator-behavior descriptions in \u00c2\u00a717.2 that reference ecm:source are correctly scoped to term objects (which declare it per \u00c2\u00a75.7), while requirements in instance-creation functional requirements (FR-U011) that reference ecm:source on the created instance are an error because \u00c2\u00a75.8 does not declare the field. The applier's before_not_found failure on C3 in task 283 (not a schema error at apply time, but a before-anchor mismatch due to apply ordering) is orthogonal to this schema-boundary concern \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the schema boundary is a ratification-time check, not an apply-time check."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "The task-282 denial included a prerequisite-ordering constraint: 'CANONICAL_RESERVED_NAME_COLLISION row must be bundled with C5 or ratified before C5 lands.' C5 landed in the partial apply (task 283). The fix-batch ratification (task 286) treats the landing of C5 as satisfying the prerequisite, allowing the \u00c2\u00a717.2 row insertion to be included in the fix batch rather than requiring a separate ratification chain. This is a candidate refinement: denial rationales that express 'bundle with X or precede X' constraints should also specify how to handle the case where X has already landed before the fix round is queued \u00e2\u20ac\u201d either the fix round automatically inherits the satisfied-prerequisite status (as done here) or a re-ratification of the prerequisite relationship is required. Making this explicit in the denial rationale would eliminate the open-question ambiguity surfaced by recon task 284."
          }
        ]
      },
