        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "Cross-component constraint sets (validator rules that must also gate UI creation) belong in a purpose-scoped module within the owning domain layer (src/validate/reserved-names.ts), not duplicated at each consumer site. The Set-at-module-load wrapper (RESERVED_CANONICAL_IRI_SET) is the correct companion pattern: it separates the canonical array declaration (human-readable, spec-citeable) from the O(1) lookup form. This pattern is directly replicable for any future validator constraint set \u00e2\u20ac\u201d e.g., DANGLING_* allowlists, MALFORMED_* shape predicates \u00e2\u20ac\u201d that must be shared with UI prevention guards without violating layer boundaries."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "When a spec defines canonical names using compact-IRI notation (e.g., rdfs:label) but the implementation stores user-entered IRI overrides verbatim without prefix expansion, the canonical-name guard must either normalize inputs before comparison or include both compact and full forms in the constraint set. The J1 chain resolves this for the full-IRI case (normative document storage form) but leaves the compact-IRI user-entry path uncovered. A forward-track candidate: add compact-IRI forms to RESERVED_CANONICAL_IRIS (making it 16 entries) or introduce an expandIri pre-pass on user input in AddTermDialog before the RESERVED_CANONICAL_IRI_SET.has() check. The trigger condition is any spec-defined canonical name list expressed in compact form where user-entry paths store verbatim input."
          }
        ]
      },
