      "outputs": {
        "findings": [
          {
            "id": "F1",
            "severity": "blocking",
            "claim": "Adjudication authority is ambiguous: the spec conflates operator-level item adjudication with architect Pass 2a ratification, naming both as occurring in Phase 05 with no clear ownership boundary.",
            "evidence": "Agent table row: 'architect | 05-adjudicate, 06-implement | Adjudication ratification decisions per atomic item; Pass 2a gating for each item's implementation chain.' Simultaneously, the operator command spec says '`state_admin feedback-round adjudicate` Records operator adjudication for a single atomic item; emits `item_adjudicated`.' The `item_adjudicated` audit event shape includes an `adjudicated_by` field that is never defined. The spec describes two distinct authority-wielding acts (operator scoping decision: ratify/amend/defer; architect structural gate: Pass 2a ruling on implementation chain) but uses the term 'adjudication' for both, assigns both to Phase 05, and leaves `adjudicated_by` undefined. An implementer cannot derive from this text who owns the item-level ratify/amend/defer decision or how operator and architect decisions sequence within Phase 05."
          },
          {
            "id": "F2",
            "severity": "blocking",
            "claim": "The `kind` field is assigned in two phases by two different actors, creating a contradictory data model.",
            "evidence": "Phase 02-decompose description (agent role section, 'feedback-decomposer' subsection): 'Each item carries: `item_id`, `parent_item_id` ... `kind` (bug-report | enhancement-request | spec-gap | editorial | data-discipline | ux-concern).' Phase table entry for 03-categorize: 'Each item has: kind classification + effort estimate (S/M/L/XL) + initial priority.' The item shape shows `kind` as a single field. If the decomposer assigns `kind`, Phase 03 is redundantly re-assigning it, creating an overwrite without a documented merge rule. If Phase 03 owns `kind` assignment, the decomposer's `kind` output is provisional or incorrect. Neither interpretation is consistent with both the agent description and the phase exit criterion. An implementation cannot determine the canonical authority for `kind` assignment."
          },
          {
            "id": "F3",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "`subject.type: feedback-round-item` extends the Spec 07 `subject.type` enumeration without acknowledging the schema extension or the required CLI change.",
            "evidence": "Spec 08, 'Integration with Spec 07 (Forward-Track Surface)' section: 'it MUST become a Spec 07 forward-track in State A... `subject.type: feedback-round-item`.' CLAUDE.md \u00c2\u00a77.9 documents `state_admin forward-track create --subject-type {banking|fixture|capability|candidacy|other}` as a closed CLI enum. `feedback-round-item` does not appear in that enum. The spec introduces the new value without noting that (a) the `--subject-type` CLI requires a code change to accept it, and (b) the Spec 07 subject-type enum definition in project/Routing/07-forward-track-surface-spec.md \u00c2\u00a7'Audit event structure for forward-tracks' lists `type: banking | fixture | capability | candidacy | other` without `feedback-round-item`. A silent extension here would cause `state_admin forward-track create` to reject the value at dispatch time."
          },
          {
            "id": "F4",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "`declaration_kind: operator_deliberate_deferral` introduces a new enum value for the `forward_track` event's `declaration_kind` field without acknowledging the schema extension.",
            "evidence": "Spec 08, 'Integration with Spec 07 (Forward-Track Surface)' section: '`declaration_kind: operator_deliberate_deferral`.' CLAUDE.md \u00c2\u00a77.12 (`state_admin promote-candidate`) establishes the only existing `declaration_kind` value: `operator_deliberate_promotion`. The draft introduces `operator_deliberate_deferral` as a sibling value without referencing any extension point in Spec 07 or the forward-track audit event schema, and without noting whether `declaration_kind` is an open string or a registered closed enum. The existing `promote-candidate` audit event shape canonicalizes `operator_deliberate_promotion`; adding a second value silently modifies the audit event schema."
          },
          {
            "id": "F5",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "The empirical motivation section inaccurately claims Events 9 and 12 from OPERATOR-MEDIATION-LOG.md inform the anti-patterns section; those events appear in neither the anti-patterns text nor any cross-reference.",
            "evidence": "Spec 08, 'Empirical motivation' section: 'OPERATOR-MEDIATION-LOG.md Events 9 and 12 document two additional substrate failure modes (wrong escalation shape via outputs.error; awaiting_operator_decision bypass not honoring the required_outputs skip specified in CLAUDE.md section 7.6). These inform the anti-patterns section.' Examining anti-patterns AP-1 through AP-5: AP-5 cites 'OPERATOR-MEDIATION-LOG.md Event 11' for the gap-16 gating risk; Events 9 and 12 do not appear in the body text of any anti-pattern. Event 9 (developer `task_too_broad` wrong escalation shape) and Event 12 (CPS required_outputs check firing despite awaiting_operator_decision) describe substrate-internal dispatch failures during a developer chain, not feedback-round processing failures. The claim that they 'inform the anti-patterns section' is not borne out by the content: no AP maps to the developer-escalation-shape failure mode. The spec's core empirical claim (J1/J2 loss per Item J in demo/FEEDBACK.md) is verified: WALKTHROUGH-PHASE-3.md confirms J1 landed (canonical-name collision row) and has no entry for J2 (starter-term population via buildNewDocument), confirming the loss. But the Events 9/12 citation is inaccurate and should be corrected or removed."
          },
          {
            "id": "F6",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "`reconciliation_status: skipped` appears in the item shape's enum but is never produced, consumed, or explained anywhere in the spec.",
            "evidence": "Item shape definition: '`reconciliation_status`: \"pending | verified | gap | skipped | null\"'. Scanning all eight phase descriptions, the seven operator commands, the seven audit event definitions, and the five anti-patterns: no phase transition, operator command, or agent operation sets `reconciliation_status` to `skipped`. The value has no documented trigger condition, no explained semantics (does `skipped` mean the item was deferred before reconciliation, or that reconciliation was waived, or something else?), and no operator path to produce it. An implementer cannot implement this value without inventing semantics the spec does not provide."
          },
          {
            "id": "F7",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "Two sections both titled 'Integration with Spec 07' cover overlapping phase-boundary sequencing material, producing structural duplication and potential implementation conflict.",
            "evidence": "Section heading 'Integration with Spec 07 (Forward-Track Surface)' contains a subsection 'Phase-boundary inheritance' stating: 'Recommended sequence at phase-close: `feedback-round close` then `forward-track inherit` then `phase-boundary`.' The immediately following top-level section 'Integration with Spec 07 (Phase-Boundary)' repeats the sequencing constraint: '`state_admin feedback-round close` MUST precede `state_admin phase-boundary`' and '`close` succeeds; `phase-boundary` may proceed.' The two sections present the same sequencing rule with different normative weight (`Recommended` in the first, `MUST` in the second) without a cross-reference. An implementer enforcing the `close`-before-`phase-boundary` constraint must decide which section's normative strength governs."
          },
