    {
      "@id": "urn:fnsr:task:306-rat-spec-08",
      "agent": "architect",
      "status": "done",
      "attempts": 1,
      "outputs": {
        "ruling": "denied",
        "editorial_verdict": "substantive",
        "editorial_verdict_reason": "The proposed change creates FNSR Protocol Spec 08 as a new surface-level specification, introducing original normative definitions for an eight-phase protocol, agent role contracts, audit event schemas, item data models, and an operator command surface \u00e2\u20ac\u201d none of which restate, reformat, or tighten existing text.",
        "rationale": "Reconnaissance (urn:fnsr:task:303-recon-spec-08) is present and thorough; the refusal is not reconnaissance-absent. The spec's core architectural insight \u00e2\u20ac\u201d three-guarantee structure (atomic decomposition before adjudication, completeness map before implementation, reconciliation after) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d is sound, and the empirical motivation (Item J's J2 provision loss confirmed by WALKTHROUGH-PHASE-3.md) is verified. However, the spec-reviewer (urn:fnsr:task:305-review-spec-08) identified two blocking structural defects, five major issues, and four supporting minor issues that collectively prevent ratification in the current form.\n\n**Blocking defects (independently confirmed)**:\n\nF1 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Adjudication authority ambiguity in Phase 05. The spec simultaneously assigns the `architect` to 'Adjudication ratification decisions per atomic item; Pass 2a gating' AND defines a `state_admin feedback-round adjudicate` command that 'Records operator adjudication for a single atomic item; emits item_adjudicated.' The audit event's `adjudicated_by` field is declared but never defined. Two distinct authority-wielding acts \u00e2\u20ac\u201d operator scoping decision (ratify/amend/defer) and architect Pass 2a structural gate on the implementation chain \u00e2\u20ac\u201d are conflated under the single term 'adjudication' with no ownership boundary, no intra-phase sequencing rule, and no defined actor set for `adjudicated_by`. An implementer cannot determine whether `adjudicated_by` means the operator, the architect, both, or something else; cannot determine whether the operator scoping decision precedes or follows the architect gate; and cannot determine whether a positive operator decision but negative architect ruling produces a net `adjudication: defer` or a net `adjudication: blocked`. This is a blocking ambiguity \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Phase 05 cannot be implemented as specified.\n\nF2 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d `kind` field contradictory ownership. The `feedback-decomposer` agent description states each item carries `kind (bug-report | enhancement-request | spec-gap | editorial | data-discipline | ux-concern)` as output. Phase 03-categorize's exit criterion states 'Each item has: kind classification + effort estimate + initial priority.' The item shape declares `kind` as a single field. No merge rule exists: if the decomposer assigns `kind`, Phase 03's exit criterion for `kind` is redundant and describes an overwrite; if Phase 03 canonically assigns `kind`, the decomposer's `kind` is provisional and must be overwritten or discarded. Neither interpretation is consistent with both the agent description and the phase table. This is a blocking data model contradiction.\n\n**Major issues (independently confirmed)**:\n\nF3 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d `subject.type: feedback-round-item` silently extends the Spec 07 closed enum. project/Routing/07-forward-track-surface-spec.md \u00c2\u00a7'Audit event structure for forward-tracks' defines `subject.type` as `banking | fixture | capability | candidacy | other`. CLAUDE.md \u00c2\u00a77.9 documents `--subject-type {banking|fixture|capability|candidacy|other}` as the CLI enum. The value `feedback-round-item` appears nowhere in either. The spec introduces the value without noting: (a) the CLI requires a code change to accept it; (b) the Spec 07 schema is being formally extended; (c) the transitional `other` value is or is not an acceptable interim placeholder. A dispatch of `state_admin forward-track create --subject-type feedback-round-item` would be rejected by the current CLI.\n\nF4 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d `declaration_kind: operator_deliberate_deferral` introduces a sibling to the sole existing `declaration_kind` value. CLAUDE.md \u00c2\u00a77.12 canonicalizes `declaration_kind: operator_deliberate_promotion` as the only existing `declaration_kind` value (from `promote-candidate`). The draft introduces `operator_deliberate_deferral` without noting whether `declaration_kind` is a closed registered enum or an open string, without referencing an extension point, and without noting the audit event schema change required.\n\nF5 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Inaccurate empirical citation in the spec body. The 'Empirical motivation' section asserts: 'OPERATOR-MEDIATION-LOG.md Events 9 and 12 document two additional substrate failure modes... These inform the anti-patterns section.' Examination of AP-1 through AP-5 shows: AP-5 cites Event 11 (architect denial not blocking applier); no anti-pattern references Events 9 or 12. Event 9 (developer used wrong escalation shape: `error: task_too_broad` instead of `awaiting_operator_decision`) and Event 12 (CPS required_outputs check fires despite awaiting_operator_decision shape, spec-implementation mismatch) describe substrate-internal dispatch failures during a developer chain, not feedback-round processing failures. The citation is factually inaccurate \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the spec's own anti-patterns section does not substantiate the claim made about it.\n\nF6 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d `reconciliation_status: skipped` is an undefined dangling enum value. The item shape specifies `reconciliation_status: 'pending | verified | gap | skipped | null'`. Across all eight phase descriptions, all ten operator commands, all seven audit events, and all five anti-patterns, no path sets `reconciliation_status` to `skipped`. There is no documented trigger condition, no agent or command that produces the value, and no semantic explanation of what `skipped` means (deferred before reconciliation? waived by operator decision? something else?). An implementer cannot implement this value without inventing semantics the spec does not provide.\n\nF7 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Two sections titled 'Integration with Spec 07' present the same sequencing constraint with contradictory normative weights. Section 'Integration with Spec 07 (Forward-Track Surface)' \u00c2\u00a7'Phase-boundary inheritance' states: 'Recommended sequence at phase-close: `feedback-round close` then `forward-track inherit` then `phase-boundary`.' Section 'Integration with Spec 07 (Phase-Boundary)' states: '`state_admin feedback-round close` MUST precede `state_admin phase-boundary`.' `Recommended` (SHOULD-strength) vs. `MUST` for the same `close`-before-`phase-boundary` constraint \u00e2\u20ac\u201d an implementer enforcing the constraint must choose between normative levels.\n\n**Minor and advisory issues** (F8\u00e2\u20ac\u201cF14, confirmed by inspection) further indicate the spec is not yet in ratifiable form but are not individually blocking. F8 (file is named `spec.md` not `surface-spec.md`, deviating from the surface convention); F9 (three non-conformant frontmatter fields with no schema registration); F10 (no provenance section, which every existing Spec 01\u00e2\u20ac\u201c07 and BAO primitive doc includes); F12 (BAO claim for `feedback-round-orchestrator` omits mandatory four-bounds enumeration per surfaces/_primitives/bounded-authority-orchestrator.md \u00c2\u00a7'The four bounds (MUST hold for every BAO instance)').\n\n**Revision path**: All blocking and major issues are enumerable and fixable. Recommended revision actions: (1) Split Phase 05 into explicitly-named sub-steps \u00e2\u20ac\u201d operator scoping decision first, architect Pass 2a gate second \u00e2\u20ac\u201d with `adjudicated_by` defined as a two-actor field; (2) resolve `kind` ownership: either decomposer assigns provisional `kind` (Phase 03 confirms/overrides with a documented merge rule) or `kind` is renamed in Phase 02 output (e.g., `provisional_kind`) with Phase 03 owning the canonical value; (3) add a 'Schema Extensions' section naming `feedback-round-item` and `operator_deliberate_deferral` as new values, noting that `other` covers the transitional CLI case, and flagging the CLI code change required; (4) correct the Events 9/12 citation \u00e2\u20ac\u201d either add an anti-pattern that maps to those events (substrate escalation-shape failures in the developer chain) or remove the citation from the empirical motivation; (5) document `skipped` semantics (e.g., 'item adjudicated `defer` before reaching Phase 07; reconciliation is not applicable') or remove the value from the enum; (6) merge the two 'Integration with Spec 07' sections into one with consistent normative weight (MUST governs).",
        "referenced_evidence": [
          {
            "type": "upstream_task",
            "id": "urn:fnsr:task:303-recon-spec-08",
