          }
        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "Phase-authority decomposition discipline for phase-gated surface specs: when a single phase contains two distinct authority-wielding acts \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a scoping decision by one actor (operator: ratify/amend/defer) and a structural gate by another (architect: Pass 2a ruling) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the spec must assign them explicitly-named sub-steps within the phase, define the sequence (scoping decision precedes gate, or gate constrains scoping), and enumerate the actor set for every `_by` field in the resulting audit event. Conflating two authority acts under one phase heading and one audit event without a boundary definition produces F1-class blocking ambiguity that cannot be resolved by implementers without inventing semantics. This pattern was observed in Spec 08 Phase 05 and corrected at ratification rather than at implementation."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "Schema extension acknowledgment protocol for new surface specs: when a proposed spec introduces new enum values that extend existing audit event schemas in upstream specs (particularly Spec 07's `subject.type` or `declaration_kind` fields), the spec must include an explicit section or subsection naming each extended schema, the new value, the existing `other`-or-equivalent transitional placeholder status, and any CLI code changes required. The Spec 07 `subject.type` enum (`banking | fixture | capability | candidacy | other`) provides `other` as a transitional placeholder by design; a new spec introducing `feedback-round-item` should note whether `other` is the interim representation or whether a formal schema extension is required, and must flag the CLI argument update. Omitting this produces F3/F4-class major issues detectable at ratification. Recommend adding a 'Schema Extensions' section to the surface-spec template used at the retro and verification surfaces."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "Phase-table \u00c3\u2014 agent-role-table \u00c3\u2014 item-shape triple-consistency check as a pre-ratification review discipline: a surface spec's three defining structures \u00e2\u20ac\u201d phase table (what exits from each phase), agent role table (what each agent emits), and item shape (the canonical field definitions) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d must be cross-checked for field assignment consistency before ratification. When two phases and one agent all claim to assign the same item field with no merge rule, the data model is contradictory; this is the F2 `kind`-field pattern observed in Spec 08. A structural review heuristic for future surface specs: for every field in the item shape, verify there is exactly one phase or agent that canonically assigns it, or an explicit documented merge rule if provisional assignment precedes canonical assignment."
          },
          {
            "id": "B4",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "Empirical citation accuracy in spec bodies: when a protocol spec's empirical motivation section cites specific operator-mediation log events as informing a named section of the spec, the spec reviewer should cross-check every cited event against the content of the named section. In Spec 08, Events 9 and 12 were cited as informing the anti-patterns section, but no anti-pattern in AP-1 through AP-5 referenced either event; AP-5 cited Event 11 instead. An inaccurate forward-reference of this kind produces F5-class major issues and weakens the spec's evidentiary grounding. Discipline: spec authors should cross-check empirical citation claims before submission; spec reviewers should treat un-grounded citation claims as major findings."
          }
        ]
      },
