        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "Two-actor adjudication field as a multi-authority phase pattern. When a protocol phase requires both content-scope adjudication (operator judgment: ratify/amend/defer) and implementation-gate adjudication (architect Pass 2a ruling), structurally separating them into distinct phases with a two-actor field ({operator: <id>, architect: <task-ref>}) prevents authority conflation and makes each actor's decision independently traceable in the audit chain. The F1 fix in Spec 08 v0.2 (Phase 05a operator scoping + Phase 05b architect gate, with adjudicated_by.operator set at 05a and adjudicated_by.architect set at 05b) is the canonical substrate instance of this pattern. The pattern is generalizable to any protocol surface where operator content judgment and architectural gate judgment are functionally distinct decisions over the same item."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "Protocol spec anti-pattern sections must not encode substrate-bug workarounds as design constraints. The v0.1 Spec 08 AP-5 proposed outputs.error: 'ratification_denied' as a 'proposed fix' for gap-16's daemon-side failure (architect denial not blocking downstream applier dispatch). This encoded a substrate bug's behavior \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the daemon checking task status rather than outputs.ruling \u00e2\u20ac\u201d as a design dependency that implementers would rely on. The v0.2 revision removes this workaround and replaces it with three elements: (a) explicit mechanism naming ('daemon checks task status rather than outputs.ruling'), (b) versioned gap label ('gap-16, v3.2 candidate'), and (c) operator-discipline framing ('operator-discipline review is a backstop \u00e2\u20ac\u201d not an architectural guarantee'). Discipline: when a spec's anti-pattern section must reference a substrate gap, document the mechanism, label it as a versioned fix candidate, and frame the v1 mitigation as operator-discipline only \u00e2\u20ac\u201d never as an architecturally-reliable design constraint."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "F-number identity drift across multi-agent ratification chains. When a reconnaissance task assigns canonical F-numbers to findings and downstream developer and reviewer tasks adopt their own numbering (e.g., developer's 'impl F6' covering skipped semantics while recon F6 covered adjudicated_by CLI gap), cross-chain traceability degrades. The developer in task 309 partially mitigated this by noting 'Recon F6 (adjudicated_by CLI --by flag) is addressed implicitly by F1' in open questions, but the reviewer in task 310 adopted the implementation numbering without aligning to recon numbering. Pattern for future chains: treat the reconnaissance task's F-numbers as canonical identifiers for the full chain; developer and reviewer passes should reference them by the recon's F-number even when implementation directions cluster them differently. The recon F-numbers are the audit-trail-visible identity of each finding from ratification backwards."
          }
        ]
      },
