        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "The operator adjudication 'UX-layer-only; no SPEC \u00c2\u00a717 amendment' for R3-S1-03 creates a deliberate and operator-sanctioned spec-implementation gap: FR-U001 retains SHOULD-level prompt language while the Chain \u00ce\u00b1 implementation enforces a MUST modal at creation time. This is a valid pattern where the UX implementation advances ahead of SPEC language within a single chain; the spec language catches up via a separate spec-amendment chain if the operator elects to formalize it. The key discipline is that the gap is operator-sanctioned (not accidental drift) and is bounded to the UX layer \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the kernel validation codes (MISSING_REALIST_ANCHOR, \u00c2\u00a717) are deliberately left unchanged as the authoritative backend/API contract."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "The handleAcknowledge path in App.tsx illustrates a structural distinction between two kinds of setValidationReport() calls that must be kept separate: (1) calls triggered by project-document mutation, where validate(updatedDoc) regenerates the full report from scratch and C6's validate-on-change pattern applies; and (2) calls triggered by user acknowledgement, where the report's acknowledged flags are mutated in-place and calling validate() would destroy those flags. The Chain \u00ce\u00b1 changes (C6, C7) correctly target only type-(1) call-sites (child component prop callbacks and dialog onSave). handleAcknowledge is type-(2) and is correctly excluded. This two-category distinction should be documented in the project's component-interaction model so future contributors do not inadvertently add a validate() call to the acknowledgement path."
          }
        ]
      },
