        "editorial_verdict": "substantive",
        "editorial_verdict_reason": "The proposed change introduces new behavioral functionality (S4-02 starter terms pre-population), creates a new source module, modifies buildNewDocument() runtime behavior, and adds test infrastructure \u00e2\u20ac\u201d all normative changes that alter what a new project document contains, well beyond any editorial correction scope.",
        "rationale": "Reconnaissance is present in UPSTREAM (urn:fnsr:task:337-recon-r3-gamma, 25 findings). Developer proposal is in UPSTREAM (urn:fnsr:task:338-dev-r3-gamma, 5 changes across 4 files). All six review-focus criteria are satisfied:\n\n(1) STARTER_TERMS list curated reasonably: 16 entries \u00e2\u20ac\u201d 8 rdfs: properties + 5 owl: properties + 3 owl: meta-classes. Within the 15-20 range. No XSD datatypes (recon F18 confirms XSD is handled separately as ecm:datatype in AddLiteralDialog).\n\n(2) Canonical IRIs + type classification + ecm:source: All 16 terms carry full canonical IRIs under http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema# (confirmed via VMP_CONTEXT prefix expansions in recon F10) and http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#. ecm:source: ecm:system-starter-example on every term. SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7 (line 465) explicitly authorizes this source value. Type classifications follow implementation_directions. The open classification questions (rdfs:isDefinedBy and rdfs:seeAlso in owl:DatatypeProperty vs owl:ObjectProperty) are semantic-sme territory and do not block architect ratification.\n\n(3) buildNewDocument deep-clone safety: C2b uses STARTER_TERMS.map((t) => ({ ...t })). The EcmTerm interface in C1 constrains every field to a primitive type (string, string literal, or null) with no nested objects or arrays. For a primitives-only interface, shallow-spread clone is semantically equivalent to structuredClone for mutation isolation \u00e2\u20ac\u201d no shared-reference hazard exists. Each buildNewDocument() call receives independent term objects.\n\n(4) Open flow preservation: handleFileChange (App.tsx:136-191 per recon F5) is untouched by C2a/C2b. User-supplied term lists from opened files are not modified.\n\n(5) J1 collision consistency: The CANONICAL_RESERVED_NAME_COLLISION check in src/validate/index.ts:204 (confirmed by direct read and recon F14) executes `if (t[\"ecm:source\"] !== \"ecm:project-created\") continue;` \u00e2\u20ac\u201d starter terms are unconditionally skipped. Validator exemption is already in place; no change required.\n\n(6) Substantive: confirmed as substantive per this verdict.\n\nAdditional structural checks passed: Lexicographic sort order in STARTER_TERMS is correct (rdfs: 2000-namespace < owl: 2002-namespace; within rdfs: comment<domain<isDefinedBy<label<range<seeAlso<subClassOf<subPropertyOf; within owl: uppercase C/D/O (ASCII 67/68/79) sort before lowercase d/e/i/s per ASCII, yielding Class<DatatypeProperty<ObjectProperty<differentFrom<equivalentClass<equivalentProperty<inverseOf<sameAs). Placement in src/validate/ follows the established reserved-names.ts pattern (imported by src/ui/App.tsx and src/ui/AddTermDialog.tsx), and allows unit-test build (tsconfig.test.json excludes src/ui) to import STARTER_TERMS directly. No kernel purity violations \u00e2\u20ac\u201d starter-terms.ts is not in src/kernel/. Epoch timestamps (EPOCH = '1970-01-01T00:00:00Z') match buildNewDocument()'s existing deterministic pattern. SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 defers rdfs:domain/range as 'first-class TBox fields'; this deferral applies to schema-level TBox modeling, not to appearance of those IRIs as ecm:system-starter-example reference vocabulary in the sidebar \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the distinction is architecturally clear. Test coverage in C3 (well-formedness: count, per-entry fields, sort order) and C4 (Playwright: New->Skip->Download->assert ecm:terms.length >= 15) is adequate for the change scope.",
        "referenced_evidence": [
