            "claim": "VERDICT confirm-aaron-correct-with-additional-detail on F14. Language-tagged rdfs:label is canonical OWL 2 usage: rdfs:label is declared with range rdfs:Literal which includes rdf:langString. Additional precision recon did not flag: the correct RDF datatype for language-tagged strings is rdf:langString, not xsd:string. The current turtle.ts:202-210 calls literal(val) producing xsd:string typed literals. After this change, the emitter must produce rdf:langString-typed values. In N3.js, literal(val, 'en') produces a language-tagged literal with datatype rdf:langString automatically \u00e2\u20ac\u201d this is the correct call.",
            "evidence": "demo/FEEDBACK-ROUND-4.md:58 (Human Label & Language Tag \u00e2\u20ac\u201d generates rdfs:label triple). src/emit/turtle.ts:202-210 (string-literal block: literal(val) for rdfs:label \u00e2\u20ac\u201d no language tag). OWL 2 Syntax Section 2.3: rdf:langString is the datatype of language-tagged strings; xsd:string is the datatype of plain strings without language tags.",
            "correction": "Term object shape: store the language tag alongside rdfs:label, e.g., add 'ecm:labelLang': string (default 'en') on term objects that carry user-input labels. In turtle.ts nodeToQuads, for the rdfs:label predicate: const lang = node['ecm:labelLang']; if (typeof val === 'string') result.push(makeQuad(subj, namedNode(expandIri('rdfs:label')), typeof lang === 'string' ? literal(val, lang) : literal(val))). Add 'ecm:labelLang' to the SEMANTIC_PREDICATE_ALLOWLIST (it is editor-asserted metadata needed for correct Turtle). Alternatively adopt the {text, lang} object shape for rdfs:label in SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7."
