          },
          {
            "id": "SA1",
            "severity": "blocking",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "ADDITIONAL STRUCTURAL ISSUE \u00e2\u20ac\u201d recon did not trace the output-pipeline consequence. The current STARTER_TERMS actively produce OWL Full (not OWL 2 DL) Turtle for every new project. Full evidence chain: (1) rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso carry type 'owl:DatatypeProperty' in STARTER_TERMS; (2) SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST in src/projection/index.ts:29-39 includes 'owl:DatatypeProperty', so these pass through projectSemantic(); (3) turtle.ts:175-178 emits rdf:type from the type field, producing the triples 'rdfs:label rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty', 'rdfs:comment rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty', 'rdfs:seeAlso rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty' in the exported Turtle; (4) OWL 2 built-in vocabulary declares these same IRIs as owl:AnnotationProperty; (5) an OWL 2 DL processor loading this Turtle encounters identical IRIs declared simultaneously as annotation property and data property \u00e2\u20ac\u201d this violates the OWL 2 DL pairwise-disjointness requirement (Structural Specification Section 3.3) and makes the ontology OWL Full. This is an active output-correctness defect affecting every project created with the current STARTER_TERMS.",
            "evidence": "src/validate/starter-terms.ts:50-58, :78-86, :95-103 (rdfs:comment/label/seeAlso typed owl:DatatypeProperty). src/projection/index.ts:29-39 (SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST includes owl:DatatypeProperty). src/emit/turtle.ts:175-178 (type field emitted as rdf:type triple). OWL 2 Structural Specification Section 3.3: 'In OWL 2 DL ontologies, the sets of object property, data property, and annotation property IRIs are required to be pairwise disjoint.' OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics Table 6.2: rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso are owl:AnnotationProperty.",
            "correction": "Primary fix (same as S4): change type of rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso entries in STARTER_TERMS from 'owl:DatatypeProperty' to 'owl:AnnotationProperty'. Parallel fix (same as SA3): add 'owl:AnnotationProperty' to SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST so annotation property terms project correctly. After both fixes, the Turtle emitter produces 'rdfs:label rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty' \u00e2\u20ac\u201d consistent with the OWL 2 built-in declaration, restoring OWL 2 DL conformance."
          },
          {
            "id": "SA2",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "ADDITIONAL STRUCTURAL ISSUE \u00e2\u20ac\u201d recon identified inappropriateness of rdfs:domain/range/subClassOf/subPropertyOf (F5/F6) but did not flag the specific type-assignment error. The canonical type of rdfs:domain, rdfs:range, rdfs:subClassOf, and rdfs:subPropertyOf in the RDFS specification is rdf:Property \u00e2\u20ac\u201d not owl:ObjectProperty. In OWL 2 DL, rdf:Property and owl:ObjectProperty are distinct metaclasses; an entity that is rdf:Property is not necessarily owl:ObjectProperty, and asserting owl:ObjectProperty entails full ABox-predicate semantics that these schema-level predicates do not have. The current STARTER_TERMS assignment of type 'owl:ObjectProperty' to these four entities creates a punning situation that, in OWL 2 DL context, is a metaclass confusion: a schema-level RDFS predicate is incorrectly represented as a user-applicable ABox predicate.",
            "evidence": "src/validate/starter-terms.ts:59-67 (rdfs:domain typed owl:ObjectProperty); :86-95 (rdfs:range); :104-120 (rdfs:subClassOf, rdfs:subPropertyOf). RDFS Specification Sections 3.3-3.5: rdfs:subClassOf rdf:type rdf:Property; rdfs:domain rdf:type rdf:Property; rdfs:range rdf:type rdf:Property; rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:type rdf:Property. OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics: these four are not listed in Table 6.2 (built-in annotation properties) or Table 6.1 (built-in object properties).",
            "correction": "Remove all four entries from STARTER_TERMS (no correct reclassification exists within the tool's type system). The tool's existing handling of rdfs:subClassOf and rdfs:subPropertyOf as structural predicates in SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7 (on class/property term objects) and in the SEMANTIC_PREDICATE_ALLOWLIST is correct and unaffected \u00e2\u20ac\u201d those are semantic-layer predicates applied to class/property nodes, not user-configurable starter vocabulary."
          },
          {
            "id": "SA3",
            "severity": "blocking",
            "category": "representation_reality",
            "claim": "ADDITIONAL STRUCTURAL ISSUE \u00e2\u20ac\u201d recon identified the EcmTermType gap (F8) but did not trace the root cause to the projection code. src/projection/index.ts:29-39 SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST does not include 'owl:AnnotationProperty'. Consequence: the projectSemantic() function, implementing the \u00c2\u00a76.3 step-2 type filter, will drop any ecm:terms entry with type 'owl:AnnotationProperty' before it reaches the Turtle emitter. User-created annotation properties are accepted by the UI, stored in the project JSON document, and then silently absent from all semantic exports (JSON-LD and Turtle). This silent data loss is a blocking correctness defect independent of the EcmTermType union gap \u00e2\u20ac\u201d fixing EcmTermType alone does not fix the export.",
            "evidence": "src/projection/index.ts:29-39 (SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST: 'owl:AnnotationProperty' absent). src/projection/index.ts:96-99 (hasSemanticType() returns false for annotation property terms, causing silent discard in projectSemantic()). src/projection/index.ts:174-178 (ENTITY_ARRAY_KEYS includes 'ecm:terms', confirming annotation property terms would be processed here if not dropped by type filter).",
            "correction": "Add 'owl:AnnotationProperty' to SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST in src/projection/index.ts:29-39: const SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST = new Set<string>(['ecm:Project', 'ecm:OntologyDesignPattern', 'owl:Class', 'owl:ObjectProperty', 'owl:DatatypeProperty', 'owl:AnnotationProperty', 'ecm:Instance', 'ecm:RelationAssertion', 'ecm:LiteralAssertion', 'ecm:Serialization']). Update SPEC \u00c2\u00a76.1 semantic type allowlist to include owl:AnnotationProperty. This is a co-required fix with S5 item 3."
          },
          {
            "id": "SA4",
            "severity": "minor",
            "category": "domain_range",
            "claim": "ADDITIONAL STRUCTURAL ISSUE \u00e2\u20ac\u201d recon did not flag rdfs:seeAlso value-type semantics. rdfs:seeAlso has range rdfs:Resource in the RDFS specification \u00e2\u20ac\u201d it is designed to link resources to related resources (typically IRI references), not to plain string literals. src/emit/turtle.ts:202-210 treats rdfs:seeAlso as a string-literal predicate, calling literal(val) for any string value. If a user provides a URI as the rdfs:seeAlso value (e.g., 'http://example.org/related'), the emitter produces 'ex:myTerm rdfs:seeAlso \"http://example.org/related\"^^xsd:string' instead of 'ex:myTerm rdfs:seeAlso <http://example.org/related>'. These are semantically distinct in RDF: a typed literal is not an IRI.",
            "evidence": "src/emit/turtle.ts:202-210 (string-literal predicates list includes 'rdfs:seeAlso'; emits literal(val) without IRI detection). RDFS Specification Section 3.3: rdfs:seeAlso rdfs:range rdfs:Resource. OWL 2 RDF-Based Semantics Section 11: annotation property values may be IRIs, typed literals, or language-tagged literals.",
            "correction": "In turtle.ts nodeToQuads, separate rdfs:seeAlso from the plain string-literal predicates block. Apply IRI detection: function isIriLike(val: string): boolean { return /^https?:\\/\\//.test(val) || /^[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:/.test(val); }. For rdfs:seeAlso: if (isIriLike(val)) result.push(makeQuad(subj, namedNode(expandIri('rdfs:seeAlso')), namedNode(expandIri(val)))); else result.push(makeQuad(subj, namedNode(expandIri('rdfs:seeAlso')), literal(val))). Alternatively, store rdfs:seeAlso values as structured objects ({ iriValue: string } | { literalValue: string }) in the Term Object shape."
          },
          {
            "id": "SA5",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "category": "bfo_cco_alignment",
            "claim": "BFO/CCO/IAO GROUNDING VERDICT. Aaron's proposed annotation property defaults (rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso) require no BFO or CCO grounding changes. These are W3C standard RDFS/OWL built-in annotation properties appropriate for any web-ontology-aligned project. The tool already has IAO grounding via obo:IAO_0000136 (iao:isAbout in turtle.ts JSON_KEY_TO_RDF_PRED). No additional BFO/CCO grounding is needed for the starter annotation property set. Minor precision issue in Aaron's Refined Mini Spec table: 'Completely ignored by semantic reasoners' is practically correct but ontologically imprecise. The accurate statement is: annotation property axioms are opaque to the OWL 2 DL consequence relation \u00e2\u20ac\u201d they do not participate in logical entailments or cause inconsistencies, but they are visible to and processed by OWL tools for documentation and metadata purposes. This imprecision does not affect implementation.",
            "evidence": "src/emit/turtle.ts:33-41 (PREFIX_MAP includes obo: and cco:). src/emit/turtle.ts:78-80 (JSON_KEY_TO_RDF_PRED maps iao:isAbout to obo:IAO_0000136). demo/FEEDBACK-ROUND-4.md:48 ('Completely ignored by semantic reasoners'). OWL 2 Structural Specification Section 11: annotation property axioms do not affect the consequence relation; they are readable by processing tools.",
            "correction": "No BFO/CCO/IAO correction required. Advisory only: if a future SPEC amendment adds formal-ontology workflow support (BFO/CCO-aligned projects), consider adding IAO:0000115 (has definition) as an optional additional starter annotation property alongside rdfs:comment. For the current general-purpose editor scope, rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso are the correct defaults. In any user-facing documentation, replace 'completely ignored by semantic reasoners' with 'not subject to logical inference \u00e2\u20ac\u201d annotation property assertions do not produce entailments or cause inconsistencies'."
          }
        ],
        "summary": "All 15 recon ontology-content findings (F4\u00e2\u20ac\u201cF18) are confirmed correct; Aaron's direction is ontologically sound throughout with no disputes. Three findings are confirmed as blocking rather than merely major: S4 (rdfs:label/comment/seeAlso typed owl:DatatypeProperty violates OWL 2 DL pairwise-disjointness of annotation and data property IRI sets), S5 (owl:AnnotationProperty absent from the type system makes the correction structurally impossible to represent), and SA3 (SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST in src/projection/index.ts:29-39 missing owl:AnnotationProperty causes silent data loss for all user-created annotation properties). Two of these blocking issues (S4 and SA1) are active output-correctness defects: the current STARTER_TERMS, as shipped, cause every new project's Turtle export to declare rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, and rdfs:seeAlso as owl:DatatypeProperty, directly conflicting with their OWL 2 built-in annotation property declarations and producing OWL Full rather than OWL 2 DL output. Two additional structural issues recon missed are added: SA2 (rdfs:domain, rdfs:range, rdfs:subClassOf, rdfs:subPropertyOf canonical type is rdf:Property not owl:ObjectProperty \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a metaclass confusion, not merely an appropriateness issue) and SA4 (rdfs:seeAlso is IRI-valued per RDFS; turtle.ts string-literal handling for it is incorrect for URI-valued usage). One minor issue with the Refined Mini Spec: the rdf: prefix in the proposed namespace dropdown is a W3C reserved prefix and must be removed (S9). BFO/CCO/IAO grounding check is clean \u00e2\u20ac\u201d no new grounding required, existing IAO reference is appropriate (SA5). The blocking issues must be resolved as a single atomic set before implementation proceeds; partial application leaves the type system inconsistent.",
