        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "When a developer change amends a canonical section that contains a pre-existing frozen-ADR violation, the developer MUST correct the violation in the same change rather than explicitly scoping it out. C3 amended \u00c2\u00a76.1 to add owl:AnnotationProperty but explicitly left the iao:OntologyDesignPattern \u00e2\u2020\u2019 ecm:OntologyDesignPattern correction (mandated by ADR-007) as 'out of scope for R4-1.' This compounded the inconsistency debt: the section was being touched anyway, the ADR was frozen and registered, the code was already updated, and amending the allowlist while preserving the wrong IRI produces a worse inconsistency state than leaving the section untouched. The discipline: section amendments that pass through known frozen-ADR violations without correcting them are structurally incomplete and subject to architect denial on that ground alone."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "The Spec 08 v0.3 semantic-SME-upstream-before-ratification pattern demonstrated measurable value in this chain. The SME review (task 349) caught two major defects \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a non-existent normative citation (S1) and a frozen-ADR IRI conflict (S2) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d before any SPEC text landed on disk. Without the SME gate, S2 would have introduced a \u00c2\u00a76.1 allowlist entry contradicting ADR-007 and live code, requiring a follow-up editorial pass and a second ratification cycle. The pattern should be formalized: for SPEC amendments that introduce or cite OWL/RDF normative sources, semantic-SME upstream of ratification is load-bearing, not advisory."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "A structural catch-22 exists when a developer batch proposes both (a) new ADR entries added to DECISIONS.md and (b) SPEC.md changes that cite those same ADR numbers in their `after` text. The _check_adr_citations CPS predicate reads the ADR registry from DECISIONS.md on disk at the time the developer task is being committed; it has no in-batch awareness of proposed DECISIONS.md additions. In principle, C6's `after` text (citing ADR-009) and C11's `after` text (whose new headers contain ADR-008 and ADR-009 parsed by _ADR_CITATION_RE) should both trigger CPS vetoes against the current registry (which ends at ADR-007). The developer task 348 appears to have passed CPS, suggesting either an in-batch resolution mechanism exists that was not visible in the code read, or the operator resolved the veto manually. The pattern warrants documentation in PLAYBOOK.md: when a batch adds new ADRs and cites them, the operator should verify CPS behavior before queuing and consider splitting DECISIONS.md and SPEC.md changes into sequential applier tasks if CPS vetoes occur."
          }
        ]
