        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "When a decomposition artifact (implementation_directions, FEEDBACK-ROUND-4-DECOMPOSITION.md) conflicts with normative SPEC prose on a specific point, the SPEC is authoritative absent an explicit SPEC amendment ratified through the standard reconnaissance \u00e2\u2020\u2019 ratification \u00e2\u2020\u2019 commit-finalize chain. The R4-STARTER-5 'Remove rdfs:isDefinedBy' entry in the decomposition document carried sub-SPEC authority and could not override SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7's normative 'they appear in starter examples' sentence naming all four annotation properties. The ratification gate is the enforcement point for this authority hierarchy: a change that conforms to decomposition directions while violating normative SPEC text must be denied until the conflict is resolved either by amending the SPEC (if the decomposition intent is correct) or correcting the implementation (if the SPEC is authoritative). Operators should explicitly resolve decomposition-vs-SPEC conflicts at the reconnaissance or developer stage; ambiguity surfacing only at the semantic-sme or ratification stage costs two additional chain slots."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "A multi-stage pipeline fix is dead-on-arrival when the patched downstream stage (turtle.ts nodeToQuads) is gated by an upstream filter (projection/index.ts applyPredicateFilter / SEMANTIC_PREDICATE_ALLOWLIST) that eliminates the input before it can reach the patch. The C10 SA4 case demonstrates this pattern: the turtle emitter change is structurally correct in isolation but has zero runtime effect because the predicate is stripped at the projection layer. Full data-flow path tracing \u00e2\u20ac\u201d from ingestion through every allowlist and filter stage to emission \u00e2\u20ac\u201d is a required discipline for any fix targeting a downstream processing node in a filter-chain architecture. When a fix requires changes at multiple pipeline stages (projection allowlist + SPEC \u00c2\u00a76.1 amendment + emitter), all stages must be included in the same change set or the fix must be explicitly deferred in its entirety; shipping half a multi-stage fix as apparently-complete code creates false confidence and obscures the unresolved defect. The reconnaissance surfaced this gap (F11/F12); the developer flagged it in open_questions; but C10 was included anyway, indicating the discipline needs reinforcement at the developer stage."
          }
        ]
      },
