      "outputs": {
        "findings": [
          {
            "id": "S1",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "representation_reality",
            "claim": "The VMP_CONTEXT lacks `\"text\": \"@value\"` and `\"lang\": \"@language\"` compact aliases. Without them, `\"rdfs:label\": {\"lang\": \"en\", \"text\": \"comment\"}` in a VMP document is interpreted by any conformant JSON-LD 1.1 processor as an object node (blank node with unknown properties `text` and `lang`), NOT as the rdf:langString literal `\"comment\"@en`. The system claims JSON-LD 1.1 as its canonical representation (SPEC header; projectSemantic() docstring: \"returns a canonical JSON-LD string\"), and the existing id\u00e2\u2020\u2019@id / type\u00e2\u2020\u2019@type compact-alias pattern explicitly establishes that VMP_CONTEXT mediates JSON-LD keyword compaction. The {text, lang} shape is a structurally correct TypeScript application object and produces correct Turtle/N-Triples output via nodeToQuads() (C12), but the JSON-LD intermediate emitted by projectSemantic() is semantically wrong for any external JSON-LD processor. Because all write paths in R4-3a produce {text,lang} on rdfs:label for ecm:terms, this gap is now system-wide.",
            "evidence": "src/kernel/canonicalize.ts:66-92 (VMP_CONTEXT \u00e2\u20ac\u201d no text/@value or lang/@language aliases defined); src/projection/index.ts:202 (projectSemantic() docstring claims JSON-LD); project/SPEC.md:7 (\"Canonical representation: JSON-LD 1.1\"); project/SPEC.md:485 (SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7 defines {text,lang} shape); UPSTREAM C4b (starter-terms now store {text:\"comment\",lang:\"en\"}); UPSTREAM C12 (nodeToQuads custom shape detection bypasses JSON-LD semantics)",
            "correction": "Add two entries to VMP_CONTEXT in src/kernel/canonicalize.ts immediately after the existing `\"type\": \"@type\"` alias: `\"lang\": \"@language\"` and `\"text\": \"@value\"`. With these aliases present, a conformant JSON-LD 1.1 processor expands `{\"text\": \"comment\", \"lang\": \"en\"}` to `{\"@value\": \"comment\", \"@language\": \"en\"}`, producing the rdf:langString literal `\"comment\"@en`. The nodeToQuads() detection logic in turtle.ts (proposed C12) does not need to change \u00e2\u20ac\u201d it reads the already-parsed JSON object whose keys are `text` and `lang`. The serialized @context block in all VMP documents will include the two new aliases; golden fixtures in Chain R4-3 must regenerate accordingly. Concrete diff to src/kernel/canonicalize.ts line 75: insert `\"lang\": \"@language\",` and `\"text\": \"@value\",` before the `\"ecm:terms\"` container definition."
          },
          {
            "id": "S2",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "other",
            "claim": "SPEC \u00c2\u00a710.4 enumerates the v0.3\u00e2\u2020\u2019v0.4 migration steps explicitly (six bullet points), but does not include rdfs:label coercion of ecm:terms entries from plain strings to {text,lang} objects. SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7 line contradicts itself: it says \"Plain-string labels from prior document versions are migrated to this shape on load (\u00c2\u00a710.4)\" but \u00c2\u00a710.4 contains no such step. The implementation (C2, migrateV3toV4) adds the coercion step and records it in transformedFields, creating a SPEC-implementation divergence in a canonical document. This is not an editorial gap \u00e2\u20ac\u201d \u00c2\u00a710.4 is normative for what v0.3 implementors must produce and test against.",
            "evidence": "project/SPEC.md:922-935 (\u00c2\u00a710.4 migration bullet list \u00e2\u20ac\u201d six steps, no rdfs:label coercion); project/SPEC.md:485 (\u00c2\u00a75.7 cross-references \u00c2\u00a710.4 for migration); UPSTREAM C2 rationale (dev flagged as open question: \"SPEC 10.4 does not enumerate this step\"); UPSTREAM open_questions[2]",
            "correction": "Add a seventh bullet to the \u00c2\u00a710.4 v0.3\u00e2\u2020\u2019v0.4 migration step list in project/SPEC.md, inserted after \"initializes ecm:serializations to []\": `- coerces plain-string \\`rdfs:label\\` values on \\`ecm:terms\\` entries to the canonical \\`{ \"text\": <string>, \"lang\": \"en\" }\\` shape per \u00c2\u00a75.7; records \\`ecm:terms[*].rdfs:label\\` in the migration report \\`transformedFields\\`.`  Also update FR-C013 in \u00c2\u00a718.1 (currently scoped to v0.2\u00e2\u2020\u2019v0.3) if SPEC intends FR-C013 to cover the full migration chain including this step, or add FR-C013a for the v0.3\u00e2\u2020\u2019v0.4 rdfs:label coercion requirement explicitly."
          },
          {
            "id": "S3",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "normalizeLabelShape (UPSTREAM C1b) accepts any non-empty string as the lang field without BCP 47 syntax validation. The passthrough branch `return raw as LabelShape` fires for any object with `text: string, lang: string`, including objects where lang is the empty string `\"\"`, a numeric string like `\"123\"`, or a structurally invalid tag like `\"not valid\"`. RDF 1.1 \u00c2\u00a72.1.6 requires: \"Literals which are language-tagged strings MUST have a language tag specified by [BCP47].\" N3.js `DataFactory.literal(text, lang)` will emit whatever string is in lang as the language tag without validation, producing an ill-formed rdf:langString triple. Although all current write paths hardcode lang:\"en\" (valid BCP 47), the exported function is callable with arbitrary input.",
            "evidence": "UPSTREAM C1b normalizeLabelShape passthrough branch: `typeof (raw as Record<string, unknown>)[\"lang\"] === \"string\"` (no format check); N3.js DataFactory.literal(value, language) trusts the caller to supply a valid BCP 47 tag; RDF 1.1 \u00c2\u00a72.1.6",
            "correction": "In normalizeLabelShape, after confirming lang is typeof string, add a minimal BCP 47 primary-subtag check before the passthrough: `const lang = (raw as Record<string, unknown>)[\"lang\"] as string; if (lang.length === 0 || !/^[a-zA-Z]{1,8}(-[a-zA-Z0-9]{1,8})*$/.test(lang)) { return { text: (raw as Record<string, unknown>)[\"text\"] as string, lang: \"en\" }; } return raw as LabelShape;` This rejects empty strings and structurally invalid tags, substituting the default \"en\" rather than allowing the invalid tag to propagate to the Turtle/N-Triples emitter."
          },
          {
            "id": "S4",
            "severity": "minor",
            "category": "representation_reality",
            "claim": "normalizeLabelShape returns {text: \"\", lang: \"en\"} for any input that is neither a plain string nor a well-formed {text:string, lang:string} object. If a stored document contains a malformed rdfs:label value on an ecm:terms entry, normalizeLabelShape produces {text:\"\", lang:\"en\"}, and the nodeToQuads() code in proposed C12 would unconditionally emit `rdfs:label \"\"@en`. The rdf:langString literal `\"\"@en` (empty lexical form, language \"en\") is a valid RDF literal per RDF 1.1, but asserting it is semantically distinct from the absence of rdfs:label: it positively claims the entity's English label is the empty string. This is an ontologically spurious assertion for terms where no usable label was recoverable.",
            "evidence": "UPSTREAM C1b normalizeLabelShape: `return { text: \"\", lang: \"en\" }` (final fallback branch); UPSTREAM C12 nodeToQuads proposed after-code: `result.push(makeQuad(subj, namedNode(expandIri(\"rdfs:label\")), literal(lv[\"text\"] as string, lv[\"lang\"] as string)))` \u00e2\u20ac\u201d no guard for empty text",
            "correction": "In the nodeToQuads rdfs:label branch (turtle.ts, proposed C12 after-code), guard the language-tagged emission against empty text: `if ((lv[\"text\"] as string).length > 0) { result.push(makeQuad(subj, namedNode(expandIri(\"rdfs:label\")), literal(lv[\"text\"] as string, lv[\"lang\"] as string))); }` This ensures `rdfs:label \"\"@en` is never asserted in the RDF output. The same guard should be applied in any other emitter that handles the {text,lang} shape (triple-narration.ts already handles empty via the `.length > 0` check at the map-set callsite; C13b is safe)."
          },
          {
            "id": "S5",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "category": "other",
            "claim": "R4-3a establishes a dual-datatype regime for rdfs:label across the VMP graph: ecm:terms entries emit rdf:langString (via {text,lang} \u00e2\u2020\u2019 literal(text,lang)); ecm:Instance entries emit xsd:string (via plain string \u00e2\u2020\u2019 literal(val)). Both datatypes are valid objects of rdfs:label (rdfs:range rdfs:Literal covers both), so no OWL 2 DL violation results. However, rdfs:label is a human-readable annotation property, and natural-language annotations canonically carry language tags (rdf:langString). The single-graph dual-datatype pattern requires downstream SPARQL consumers to union `rdfs:label` filters for both xsd:string and rdf:langString, creating query complexity. ADR-008 explicitly scopes {text,lang} to ecm:terms only; this finding surfaces the ontological cost of that scoping decision for downstream consumers.",
            "evidence": "ADR-008 (project/DECISIONS.md:137-150): \"All term objects in ecm:terms store rdfs:label as {text,lang}\"; UPSTREAM C12 comment: \"plain string (ecm:Instance, legacy) -> untagged literal; {text,lang} object (ecm:terms, ADR-008) -> language-tagged rdf:langString\"; RDF 1.1 \u00c2\u00a72.1.6 on rdf:langString; SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.8 (instance shape \u00e2\u20ac\u201d rdfs:label as plain string)",
            "correction": "No correction required for R4-3a scope \u00e2\u20ac\u201d ADR-008 is ratified. Queue as a future chain item: extend ADR-008 scope to ecm:Instance.rdfs:label, updating SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.8 to define `\"rdfs:label\": { \"text\": <string>, \"lang\": <BCP-47-tag> }` for instances as well, and update all instance-write paths (AddInstanceDialog, EditInstanceDialog if present), buildInstanceLabelMap in label-resolution.ts, and the plain-string branch in nodeToQuads. This unifies the rdfs:label datatype across the full graph."
          }
        ],
        "summary": "The R4-3a proposed changes correctly implement the {text,lang} shape at the application layer: normalizeLabelShape is idempotent (plain string \u00e2\u2020\u2019 {text,lang}; {text,lang} \u00e2\u2020\u2019 same reference; malformed \u00e2\u2020\u2019 empty fallback), normalizeOnLoad correctly applies label coercion before the structural serializeVmp comparison, migrateV3toV4 adds coercion, all UI read paths gain resolveTermLabel, and turtle.ts C12 correctly splits rdfs:label handling to emit literal(text,lang) via N3.js \u00e2\u20ac\u201d which per RDF 1.1 produces a valid rdf:langString triple with BCP 47 tag 'en'. The N-Triples emitter inherits the fix via the shared graphToQuads(). No new OWL 2 DL violations are introduced. Two major gaps require revision before acceptance: (1) VMP_CONTEXT does not declare 'text'\u00e2\u2020\u2019'@value' and 'lang'\u00e2\u2020\u2019'@language' compact aliases, so the JSON-LD intermediate from projectSemantic() is semantically incorrect for conformant JSON-LD 1.1 processors \u00e2\u20ac\u201d rdfs:label on terms is interpreted as a blank-node reference rather than an rdf:langString literal; (2) SPEC \u00c2\u00a710.4 migration step list omits the rdfs:label coercion step despite SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7 cross-referencing \u00c2\u00a710.4 for it. Two lesser findings: the normalizeLabelShape lang field is not BCP 47 validated (advisory), and the empty-text fallback can produce rdfs:label \"\"@en (minor, guard needed in nodeToQuads).",
