      "outputs": {
        "findings": [
          {
            "id": "S1",
            "severity": "blocking",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "VMP_CONTEXT in src/kernel/canonicalize.ts does not declare `rdfs:range` as an IRI-typed property. Without `\"rdfs:range\": { \"@type\": \"@id\" }` in the @context, a conformant JSON-LD 1.1 processor interprets `\"rdfs:range\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal\"` as a plain string literal, producing the RDF triple `<termIRI> rdfs:range \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal\"^^xsd:string` instead of `<termIRI> rdfs:range xsd:decimal`. A literal object is not a DataRange; this renders the OWL 2 DL range axiom semantically void in the canonical JSON-LD format. The Turtle emitter (C7) correctly uses namedNode for the range value via its own PREFIX_MAP expansion, but the SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.2 canonical JSON-LD is the normative representation. Every other IRI-valued predicate in VMP_CONTEXT (`rdfs:subClassOf`, `rdfs:subPropertyOf`, `ecm:subjectIri`, `ecm:classIris`, `iao:isAbout`, etc.) carries `@type: \"@id\"`; `rdfs:range` is the sole IRI-valued predicate that C8 leaves untyped. This gap is new to R4-4: before ADR-009, no `rdfs:range` values were stored, so the missing context entry had no effect.",
            "evidence": "src/kernel/canonicalize.ts:66-94 (VMP_CONTEXT; rdfs:range absent entirely; contrast rdfs:subClassOf at line 92: { \"@type\": \"@id\", \"@container\": \"@set\" }); UPSTREAM C8 (adds foaf/schema prefix mappings but omits rdfs:range @type declaration); SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.2 (normative @context); SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 (rdfs:range value is an IRI pointing to a DataRange)",
            "correction": "Extend C8 in src/kernel/canonicalize.ts to add the following entry to VMP_CONTEXT, placed after the `\"rdfs:subPropertyOf\": { \"@type\": \"@id\", \"@container\": \"@set\" }` line: `\"rdfs:range\": { \"@type\": \"@id\" }` \u00e2\u20ac\u201d single-valued (no `@container: @set`), consistent with the SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.7 example showing `rdfs:range` as a singular string value. The VMP_CONTEXT entry for `obo:` must remain as-is; this addition is solely to `rdfs:range`."
          },
          {
            "id": "S2",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "The SPEC \u00c2\u00a76.1 mandates: 'The validator MUST emit an `ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION` finding (severity: error) on violation' of the OWL 2 DL property partition constraint (IRI sets of owl:AnnotationProperty, owl:DatatypeProperty, and owl:ObjectProperty MUST be pairwise-disjoint). This code is absent from src/validate/codes.ts and the corresponding check is absent from src/validate/index.ts. In OWL 2 DL, the same IRI cannot simultaneously be declared as both owl:DatatypeProperty and owl:AnnotationProperty (or owl:ObjectProperty); this violates the entity-type partition condition in OWL 2 DL \u00c2\u00a73.1. The PropertyCreationDialog (C1) exposes both owl:AnnotationProperty and owl:DatatypeProperty creation paths with user-controlled namespace+localname IRI composition; the IRI uniqueness check is deliberately non-blocking (emits a warning but does not prevent submit). A user who ignores the duplicate-IRI warning can create a second property term with the same IRI but a different type, producing an OWL 2 DL-invalid ontology that the validator will not flag. The R4-4 chain adds RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY (C9) but does not add ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION, leaving the pairwise-disjointness guarantee formally unenforceable at validation time.",
            "evidence": "src/validate/codes.ts:13-55 (ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION absent; 17 hard-error codes listed; RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY is proposed in C9 but ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION is not); SPEC \u00c2\u00a76.1 line 730 (normative MUST); UPSTREAM C1 PropertyCreationDialog.tsx: iriAlreadyExists warning is non-blocking; OWL 2 DL Structural Specification \u00c2\u00a73.1 (entity-type partition for properties)",
            "correction": "1. Add to src/validate/codes.ts section 17.2: `export const ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION = \"ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION\";`\n2. Add to src/validate/index.ts, after the CANONICAL_RESERVED_NAME_COLLISION block and before the RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY block, a validator that builds three Sets of term IRIs by type (owl:AnnotationProperty, owl:DatatypeProperty, owl:ObjectProperty) by iterating rawTerms, then emits makeError(ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION, ..., termIri) for every IRI that appears in more than one of those sets. Import ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION from ./codes.js in the same import amendment as RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY (C10)."
          },
          {
            "id": "S3",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "The RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY validator in C11 fires on any `rdfs:range` value that does not start with `http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#` or `xsd:`. The SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 normative trigger is narrower and structurally different: 'when an owl:DatatypeProperty term carries a rdfs:range value that resolves to an IRI typed as owl:Class in ecm:terms.' These two checks are not equivalent. (A) The XSD-prefix heuristic produces false positives for valid OWL 2 DL datatypes that are not XSD-namespaced: `owl:real`, `owl:rational`, `rdf:PlainLiteral`, `rdfs:Literal`, and any custom `rdfs:Datatype` instance defined in an imported ontology. All are legal DataRange values in OWL 2 DL; all would trigger RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY under C11. (B) The heuristic fails to perform the SPEC-mandated lookup: it does not check whether the range IRI is actually typed as `owl:Class` in `ecm:terms`. A class IRI imported from an external ontology and not present in `ecm:terms` would be flagged by the heuristic (correct behaviour, achieved accidentally); a project-created class IRI that starts with `xsd:` (pathological but not impossible in a hand-crafted document) would not be flagged (incorrect). The SPEC's normative trigger is a lookup predicate, not a string-prefix predicate. For the current UI-only path (PropertyCreationDialog dropdown of 8 XSD types), neither false positive nor false negative is reachable, but the validator is general-purpose and will be exercised against imported ontologies.",
            "evidence": "UPSTREAM C11 src/validate/index.ts proposed change: `const isXsd = rangeVal.startsWith(XSD_NS_FULL) || rangeVal.startsWith(\"xsd:\"); if (!isXsd) { findings.push(...) }`; SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 line 817: 'when an owl:DatatypeProperty term carries a rdfs:range value that resolves to an IRI typed as owl:Class in ecm:terms'; OWL 2 Specification \u00c2\u00a74.4 (DataRange; owl:real, owl:rational, rdf:PlainLiteral as valid built-in datatypes outside XSD namespace)",
            "correction": "Replace the XSD-prefix heuristic in C11 with a two-pass ecm:terms lookup: (1) Build `const classIriSet = new Set<string>()` by iterating rawTerms and adding each IRI whose `type === \"owl:Class\"`. (2) For each owl:DatatypeProperty term where `rdfs:range` is a non-empty string, check `if (classIriSet.has(rangeVal))` and only then emit makeError(RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY, ...). The XSD-prefix guard `!isXsd` is removed entirely; valid non-XSD OWL DataRange values pass through without a finding. This matches the SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 normative trigger exactly."
          },
          {
            "id": "S4",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "category": "other",
            "claim": "BCP47_RE = `/^[a-zA-Z]{2,8}(-[a-zA-Z0-9]{1,8})*$/` requires the primary language subtag to be 2\u00e2\u20ac\u201c8 letters. RFC 5646 \u00c2\u00a72.1 and \u00c2\u00a72.2.1 define two additional valid primary-subtag forms: (a) the private-use singleton `x` (a single letter, not 2\u00e2\u20ac\u201c8), which must be followed by at least one extension subtag (e.g., `x-my-private`); (b) grandfathered irregular tags whose primary subtag is the singleton `i` (e.g., `i-klingon`). Both are valid BCP 47 tags and would be rejected by the current regex with a validation error, producing a false 'invalid language tag' message. For ontology-modeling use cases these forms are rare but not impossible (e.g., a privately-coined ontology term annotated with a private-use language tag for a constructed language). The issue is confined to the UI form; the Turtle emitter and validator do not use the regex and are unaffected.",
            "evidence": "UPSTREAM C1 src/ui/PropertyCreationDialog.tsx: `const BCP47_RE = /^[a-zA-Z]{2,8}(-[a-zA-Z0-9]{1,8})*$/`; RFC 5646 \u00c2\u00a72.1 (primary subtag length: 2\u00e2\u20ac\u201c8 alpha for language subtags; singleton `x` for private use); IANA Language Subtag Registry (grandfathered `i-*` tags)",
            "correction": "Replace the regex literal with: `const BCP47_RE = /^([a-zA-Z]{2,8}|[xXiI](-[a-zA-Z0-9]{1,8})+)(-[a-zA-Z0-9]{1,8})*$/;` \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the alternation permits either a 2\u00e2\u20ac\u201c8 letter normal primary subtag OR a single-letter `x`/`i` primary followed by at least one mandatory extension subtag, then zero or more additional subtags for both branches."
          }
        ],
        "summary": "Four ontological findings across the R4-4 Property Creation Module changes. One is blocking: the VMP_CONTEXT (C8) adds foaf/schema namespace prefixes but omits `\"rdfs:range\": { \"@type\": \"@id\" }`, leaving the canonical JSON-LD representation of DatatypeProperty range axioms semantically incorrect \u00e2\u20ac\u201d conformant JSON-LD processors would treat the XSD IRI as a plain string literal rather than an IRI reference, voiding the OWL 2 DL axiom in the normative format. Two are major: (a) ANNOTATION_DATATYPE_IRI_COLLISION is absent from the validator despite the SPEC \u00c2\u00a76.1 mandate, leaving pairwise property-type disjointness unenforced at validation time while the new dialog's non-blocking IRI uniqueness check cannot substitute for this; (b) the RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY validator trigger in C11 uses an XSD-prefix string heuristic rather than the SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 normative `owl:Class`-in-ecm:terms lookup, producing false positives for valid OWL 2 DL datatypes outside the XSD namespace (owl:real, rdf:PlainLiteral, etc.). One is advisory: the BCP 47 regex rejects valid private-use (`x-...`) and grandfathered (`i-...`) primary subtags. The AnnotationProperty default, the radio-button mutual-exclusion guarantee within a single term, the Turtle emission logic (C7), and the IRI composition approach are all correct.",
