            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "When a new IRI-valued predicate is introduced to the data model (here rdfs:range, authorized by ADR-009), two independent serialization paths must both be patched: (1) the Turtle emitter's IRI-valued predicates loop (which resolves IRIs via PREFIX_MAP) and (2) the JSON-LD @context (which requires `\"predicate\": { \"@type\": \"@id\" }` to tell conformant JSON-LD processors to treat the value as an IRI rather than a string literal). Developers can correctly wire the Turtle path and add new namespace prefix entries to VMP_CONTEXT while missing the `@type: @id` entry for the predicate itself \u00e2\u20ac\u201d because the two expansions are structurally different (PREFIX_MAP handles namespace compression; `@type: @id` handles value-type discrimination). The S1 finding in urn:fnsr:task:370-sme-r4-4 instantiates this pattern exactly. A checklist item for any developer task that adds an IRI-valued predicate: 'Add `\"<predicate>\": { \"@type\": \"@id\" }` to VMP_CONTEXT in addition to any namespace prefix entries.'"
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "Validator trigger conditions expressed in SPEC normative prose as data-lookup predicates MUST be implemented as Set lookups against the project's entity collections \u00e2\u20ac\u201d not approximated by string-prefix heuristics. SPEC \u00c2\u00a77.5 specifies RANGE_CLASS_ON_DATATYPE_PROPERTY fires \"when an owl:DatatypeProperty term carries a rdfs:range value that resolves to an IRI typed as owl:Class in ecm:terms\" \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a lookup predicate over ecm:terms. Developer C11 implemented an XSD-namespace string-prefix guard instead, which produces false positives for valid OWL 2 DL datatypes outside the XSD namespace (owl:real, owl:rational, rdf:PlainLiteral, rdfs:Literal, custom rdfs:Datatype instances). The discipline: when the SPEC says 'resolves to a type in ecm:terms,' the implementation is `classIriSet.has(rangeVal)`, not `rangeVal.startsWith('xsd:')`. String-prefix guards are permissible as performance shortcuts only when the SPEC's normative trigger is itself prefix-based \u00e2\u20ac\u201d which \u00c2\u00a77.5 is not."
          }
        ]
      },
