      "outputs": {
        "findings": [
          {
            "id": "Q-R5-X",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "representation_reality",
            "claim": "The coworker's framing of 'CCO-compliant domain graph vs. modeler interchange graph' is a false dichotomy. The correct ontological decomposition is: which vocabulary layer belongs in which output surface. The ecm:* vocabulary is itself legitimately CCO-anchored (ecm:OntologyDesignPattern rdfs:subClassOf cco:ont00000958); it is not a departure from CCO. The problem is that tool-internal bookkeeping terms (ecm:Instance, ecm:isSerializationOf as currently emitted without justification) appear alongside domain content (Book, Author, Publisher, Dune, etc.) in the consumer-facing Turtle export. Option gamma is correct, but its justification must be grounded in layer separation \u00e2\u20ac\u201d domain-content vs. tool-metadata \u00e2\u20ac\u201d not in a 'compromise' of CCO discipline.",
            "evidence": "src/tbox/index.ts:24-50 (ecm:* TBox all subClassOf cco:ont00000958), src/emit/turtle.ts:66-72 (TBOX_IDS does not include ecm:Instance), src/projection/index.ts:36 (ecm:Instance in SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST), demo/library-catalog.jsonld:74-117 (every instance typed ecm:Instance in exported graph)",
            "correction": "Select Option gamma. Implementation consequences: (1) Internal JSON-LD project files retain ecm:Instance as a type discriminator (needed by the projection layer and UI). (2) src/emit/turtle.ts nodeToQuads() must skip ecm:Instance when emitting rdf:type quads for non-TBox nodes. (3) demo/library-catalog.jsonld fixture Book class gets rdfs:subClassOf cco:ont00000064. (4) obo:IAO_0000136 is retained as the aboutness predicate (not replaced with cco:ont00001808) per the canonical-source-IRI principle. (5) ecm:isSerializationOf is retained with a skos:definition added. The consumer-vs-internal split is precisely modeled by the surface-audience primitive: the Turtle download is consumer audience; the JSON-LD project file is internal audience.",
            "sme_verdict": "confirm-with-detail",
            "sme_verdict_note": "Option gamma is the ontologically correct choice, but not because it is a 'compromise.' The ecm:* vocabulary is a legitimate CCO-grounded modeler vocabulary. The real issue is that tool-bookkeeping IRIs (ecm:Instance as a type of domain individuals) are leaking into consumer-facing output. Under Option gamma, the consumer-facing Turtle expresses pure domain content anchored in CCO; the internal JSON-LD retains the tool vocabulary needed by the UI and projection layer. This maps cleanly to the surface-audience primitive. The SME discipline rejects conflating 'CCO compliance' with 'remove all ecm: terms' \u00e2\u20ac\u201d ecm:OntologyDesignPattern and ecm:Serialization ARE CCO-compliant ICE subclasses and belong in the TBox. Only ecm:Instance as a per-individual domain type is the offender."
          },
          {
            "id": "R5-A1a",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "category": "naming_iri",
            "claim": "No ontology content is changed by this item. IRI-syntax constraint flagged for the forthcoming free-text prefix input: (1) The namespace IRI must end with '#' or '/' to compose valid absolute IRIs from prefix+localName. (2) The custom prefix string should be validated as an XML NCName (allowing underscore start and body underscores), not the narrower pattern suggested in the decomposition. (3) The current LOCAL_NAME_RE in PropertyCreationDialog.tsx is correct for Turtle PN_LOCAL production and should not be narrowed.",
            "evidence": "src/ui/PropertyCreationDialog.tsx:50-54 (NAMESPACE_OPTIONS hardcoded with correct trailing slashes), src/ui/PropertyCreationDialog.tsx:74 (LOCAL_NAME_RE = /^[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_.-]*$/)",
            "correction": "When implementing the free-text prefix affordance: validate the custom namespace IRI with /^(https?:\\/\\/[^\\s]+[#\\/]|urn:[^\\s]+#)$/ (minimal guard requiring trailing # or /). Validate the custom prefix label with /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$/ (NCName-compatible; allows underscore start). Do not adopt the decomposition's narrower suggestion of /^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9-]*$/ which incorrectly excludes underscore-start prefixes that are legal in Turtle.",
            "sme_verdict": "confirm-with-detail",
            "sme_verdict_note": "Pure-UX item; no ontology semantics change. SME flag is limited to IRI-syntax: the composed IRI is ontologically significant (it becomes the property's canonical IRI in the exported graph), so the namespace IRI must be a valid absolute IRI with correct fragment/path delimiters. The existing LOCAL_NAME_RE is correct. The decomposition's suggested regex for the prefix label is overly restrictive compared to XML NCName production."
          },
          {
            "id": "R5-A1b",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "category": "naming_iri",
            "claim": "Forward-track item. When the ontology-import feature is scoped, the import contract must correctly distinguish prefix strings (presentational; no OWL 2 semantics) from namespace IRIs (semantically significant). Prefix-auto-population from imported ontologies must extract the IRI binding (the namespace IRI) from the ontology's @prefix declarations, not infer it from human-readable labels or ontology names.",
            "evidence": "demo/FEEDBACK-ROUND-5-DECOMPOSITION.md:78-82 (A1b scope)",
            "correction": "Forward-track requirement for the import-feature scope: the import contract schema must include a field for namespace IRI bindings derived from the imported ontology's own @prefix (Turtle), xmlns: (RDF/XML), or @context (JSON-LD) declarations. The UI prefix-population must bind the prefix label to the canonical namespace IRI from the imported ontology, not construct one heuristically. An SME review of the import contract is required at that scope per Spec 08 v0.3 AP-7.",
            "sme_verdict": "confirm-with-detail",
            "sme_verdict_note": "No now-scope verdict needed. Forward-track note: prefix names in OWL 2 are purely presentational metadata (RDF syntax sugar). Two serializations of the same ontology may use different prefix strings for the same namespace IRI \u00e2\u20ac\u201d this is not a conformance issue. The only thing that matters ontologically is the namespace IRI. Any import-feature prefix-population scheme that forgets this will silently create IRI collisions if two imported ontologies assign different prefix labels to the same namespace IRI."
          },
          {
            "id": "R5-A2",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "representation_reality",
            "claim": "Confirmed. The library-catalog.jsonld fixture's ecm:terms array contains 7 entries (3 owl:Class, 2 owl:ObjectProperty, 2 owl:DatatypeProperty) and none of the three starter annotation properties (rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso). Loading this file produces a project missing the starter-terms invariant. The defect is at the upload-deserialization path, not the fixture itself.",
            "evidence": "demo/library-catalog.jsonld:218-261 (ecm:terms array; IRIs present are urn:uuid:...cccccccc0001 through 0007 only; no rdfs: IRIs), src/validate/starter-terms.ts:41-71 (STARTER_TERMS canonical shape with ecm:source: ecm:system-starter-example, ecm:createdAt: EPOCH, ecm:ontologyId: null)",
            "correction": "In the project-load handler: after deserializing, iterate over STARTER_TERMS. For each entry, check whether ecm:terms already contains an entry with matching id. If absent, inject the full STARTER_TERMS entry verbatim \u00e2\u20ac\u201d do NOT reconstruct; use the STARTER_TERMS constant. Re-sort ecm:terms by id after injection. Critical: ecm:createdAt MUST be EPOCH (1970-01-01T00:00:00Z), not new Date().toISOString(). Injecting with a live timestamp would produce non-deterministic serializations of uploaded projects (violating SPEC \u00c2\u00a79 / ADR-001 determinism constraint).",
            "sme_verdict": "confirm-with-detail",
            "sme_verdict_note": "Claim confirmed by direct inspection of the fixture. The critical detail is timestamp determinism: STARTER_TERMS uses EPOCH as the canonical creation timestamp for system-issued terms. If the upload-path injection uses a live timestamp, it violates the determinism guarantee (same document loaded twice would produce different canonical forms). The injected terms must match STARTER_TERMS exactly, including ecm:source: ecm:system-starter-example and ecm:ontologyId: null."
          },
          {
            "id": "R5-A3",
            "severity": "major",
            "category": "dl_conformance",
            "claim": "Confirmed. OWL 2 DL defines two syntactically and semantically distinct axiom types: AnnotationAssertion(AP subject value) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d annotation properties, no semantic entailments for reasoners; and DataPropertyAssertion(DP individual literalValue) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d data properties, participate in datatype reasoning. The current Inspector conflates both under one section. rdfs:label is typed owl:AnnotationProperty in STARTER_TERMS and its assertion is formally an AnnotationAssertion; it must move to the Annotation Assertion section.",
            "evidence": "src/validate/starter-terms.ts:49-70 (rdfs:label, rdfs:comment, rdfs:seeAlso all typed owl:AnnotationProperty), src/ui/Inspector.tsx:55-70 (EcmInstance type guard; single label field at type level), demo/FEEDBACK-ROUND-5.md:36-43 (A3 request)",
            "correction": "In the Instance inspector: partition ecm:LiteralAssertions whose ecm:predicateIri resolves to a term in ecm:terms by the term's 'type' field. Section 1 (Annotation Assertion): predicateIri resolves to owl:AnnotationProperty term or is a known STARTER_TERMS IRI. Section 2 (DataType Assertion): predicateIri resolves to owl:DatatypeProperty term. The rdfs:label entry is rendered within the Annotation Assertion section \u00e2\u20ac\u201d not as a special top-of-inspector Label input. The partition lookup must check ecm:terms by id, falling back to STARTER_TERMS for system properties.",
            "sme_verdict": "confirm",
            "sme_verdict_note": "The split is exactly correct per OWL 2 DL. Annotation assertions have no semantic entailments in OWL 2; data property assertions carry typed-literal semantics and participate in reasoning. Presenting them in the same section incorrectly suggests semantic equivalence. The rdfs:label relocation is not cosmetic \u00e2\u20ac\u201d it enforces the ontological commitment that rdfs:label is an annotation (owl:AnnotationProperty) and its value is a non-semantic decoration, not a data assertion. This is consistent with the Round 4 SA1 fix that correctly typed rdfs:label as owl:AnnotationProperty."
          },
