          },
          {
            "type": "spec_section",
            "id": "\u00c2\u00a7Reconnaissance requirement",
            "source": "FNSR Spec 03"
          },
          {
            "type": "spec_section",
            "id": "AP-7",
            "source": "Spec 08 v0.3"
          }
        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "The developer agent's use of a structured `tests_recommended` output field \u00e2\u20ac\u201d listing the target test file path and a describe-string for each recommended test case \u00e2\u20ac\u201d makes test-debt explicit and auditable at the same moment the feature is proposed, rather than burying it in prose rationale or open_questions text. When a feature-scope change is submitted without corresponding test updates (a recognized gap), this field gives the operator a machine-readable record of what tests are owed and where, making the debt trackable across the audit chain. Formalizing this as a developer-agent output discipline (structured test-debt field on changes that do not include test file updates) would improve gap-tracking across implementation chains."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "The proposed implementation consistently follows a two-tier validation pattern for conditional form inputs: (1) soft derived booleans (customNsIriValid, customPrefixLabelValid) are true when the field is empty, false only on invalid non-empty content \u00e2\u20ac\u201d these drive inline error display and do not alarm the user before they have typed anything; (2) hard submit guards require non-empty values and regex conformance before allowing submission. This pattern is already established in the existing form (localNameValid, langTagValid follow the same soft convention) and is extended consistently by C4 and C7. The pattern distinguishes 'don't alarm on blank' from 'refuse to submit incomplete data' in a way that is architecturally explicit in the derived-boolean declarations rather than implicit in imperative checks. Future agents adding conditional inputs to this or analogous dialogs should follow the same convention to maintain intra-component consistency."
          }
        ]
      },
