        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "The SME's 'partition lookup must check ecm:terms by id, falling back to STARTER_TERMS for system properties' instruction was correctly translated into a two-tier helper (getTermTypeByIri seeded with STARTER_TERMS then overridden by ecm:terms entries). This seeding-with-override pattern is a reusable shape whenever the substrate's system-issued terms and user-extensible terms both contribute to a lookup space; the developer chose ecm:terms-overrides-STARTER_TERMS ordering, which matches the project-extends-system semantics established by the starter-terms injection path in upload-deserialization (R5-A2)."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "The developer correctly preserved data-testid='gw-inspector-instance-label-input' at its new DOM location rather than renaming it (recon F8 had explicitly raised this as an open question; recon listed three test files asserting this id by name). Preserving the testid across a UI relocation keeps the three instance-label.spec.ts tests structurally green while a new ancestor-descendant assertion (`gw-inspector-annotation-assertions` contains the input) can be added in a separate test chain. This is the canonical pattern for relocating a load-bearing input under a semantic re-partition: keep the leaf testid stable, add new section-testids, and let test additions lock in the new structural invariant separately from the structural change itself."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "R5-A3 is the first concrete realization in the GraphWrite UI of an OWL 2 DL axiom-type distinction (AnnotationAssertion vs DataPropertyAssertion) made visible at the rendering boundary. The SME's note that 'presenting them in the same section incorrectly suggests semantic equivalence' is the load-bearing rationale \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the UI partition is not cosmetic but enforces an ontological commitment that downstream reasoners depend on. Future SME-reviewed UI items that surface DL distinctions (e.g., owl:ObjectProperty vs owl:AnnotationProperty assertion targets, individual-vs-class typing) should follow the same SME-then-developer-then-ratification chain shape used here."
          }
        ]
