              ],
              "summary": "Phase 2 exit QA surfaces two primary risk clusters. First, defect distribution: the largest concentration of defects was in the ontology-content layer (starter-terms type assignments, SEMANTIC_TYPE_ALLOWLIST coverage, fixture @context freshness) and the export/upload pipeline. All four sprint-blocking defects (SA1, SA3, R3-S5-01, R5-A2) were caught by stakeholder or SME review, not by automated tests. The snapshot golden-file pattern (QA-3) is a structural false-pass risk: validating code against code-generated output creates self-consistent tests that cannot detect semantic incorrectness against external canonical sources. Two of these defects (R5-A2 and SME-R5-01) were open at Phase 2 close. Second, verification-infrastructure: test-runner friction manifested as 4 distinct operator-action recovery events at a single task's verification step (Events 4\u00e2\u20ac\u201c7), Playwright is structurally decoupled from npm test, and the Pass 2a gating enforcement gap (QA-6) allowed 3 architect denials to proceed to applier before the daemon fix. Spec 08 v0.3's content-domain-based SME routing (QA-8) is the highest-leverage process correction of the sprint \u00e2\u20ac\u201d it directly addresses the root cause of the two blocking defects. Looking forward, validator coverage at 4 of 26 codes (QA-9) is the highest-leverage test investment for Phase 3 given the expanded validation surface. Two minor observations (QA-11 decomposition scope-field error; QA-12 fixture shape discrepancy) warrant operator clarification but have bounded risk."
            }
