        "summary": "Phase 2 (Browser UI Foundation) executed 5 stakeholder feedback rounds over multi-week elapsed time, producing 362 done tasks and 121 currently-blocked tasks in the audit chain. The defining delivery pattern is the Round 5 recon-front cascade: four sequential cascade rebuilds (v2 applier-deps veto, v3 C6-retry + WinError 2 block, v4 npm-cmd fix, v5 recon-front deadlock) consumed approximately 71 replacement task slots for 7 implementation chains. Each cascade trigger was distinct and orthogonal \u00e2\u20ac\u201d graph-correctness miss, partial-apply overlap, Windows subprocess resolution failure, recon-dep deadlock \u00e2\u20ac\u201d meaning no single upstream fix would have prevented all four. The 124 operator_reset events in the full audit chain are the clearest coordination overhead metric: each represents a manual dispatch intervention, with roughly 39+ concentrated in two burst clusters within Round 5 alone. Blocker distribution clusters at two structural points: applier-side (35 apply_partial_failure events, each immediately veto-cascading downstream) and test-runner-side (WinError 2, verification-step coverage gaps, Events 4-8 in OPERATOR-MEDIATION-LOG). The phase has no declared lifecycle state machine: ROADMAP reads 'Phase 2 Not Started' despite multi-week delivery, confirmed as SVG-1.1 drift evidence in state-verification-gate.md. Sprint predictability is therefore inference-only from task timestamp archaeology rather than first-class audit events. Pre-dispatch coordination overhead for Round 5 is also non-trivial: Spec 08 v0.3 mandatory SME upstream review (task 374) added a planning cycle that caught two scope-file errors (SME-R5-02) and one hidden defect (SME-R5-01) before any implementation queued, but the same cycle gates dispatch; additionally, the foundational Q-R5-X adjudication blocks 4 of 10 now-scope chains until a single operator decision is recorded."
