            "id": "RA-1",
            "title": "state_admin write commands have no lock acquisition, creating a live write-race against the daemon's polling loop",
            "severity": "major",
            "kind": "systemic_fragility",
            "evidence": [
              "state_admin.py:70-81 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d _load_state() opens state.jsonld with plain open(); _save_state() writes via os.replace() with no call to _acquire_lock or the locked_state() context manager",
              "fnsr_daemon.py:100-112 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d locked_state() acquires the lock on state.jsonld.lock, then reads, yields, and writes; this guard is entirely absent from state_admin's read/write path",
              "PLAYBOOK.md:937 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d 'Always stop the daemon first (Ctrl-C) to avoid race conditions. The daemon's lock is on state.jsonld.lock, not the data file.' \u00e2\u20ac\u201d confirming state_admin does NOT participate in the lock protocol",
              "state.jsonld:59416 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d banking id bank-484-test-r5b-add-annotation-1 explicitly surfaced as 'state_admin write-race vs daemon polling'; open question in retro QA output: 'is this a latent test-infrastructure risk that could produce spurious failures in CI environments?'",
              "state.jsonld:59629 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d QA agent open question confirms the race is unresolved: 'or was the race confirmed as session-only and bounded to the specific run context?'"
            ],
            "rationale": "The daemon's locked_state() acquires an OS-level lock on state.jsonld.lock before reading or writing state.jsonld. state_admin.py's _load_state/_save_state bypass this lock entirely. When the operator runs any state_admin write command (phase implementing, bank, retro phase-transition, promote-candidate) while the daemon is alive in its 2-second polling loop, a classic read-modify-write race exists: both processes read version N, state_admin writes version N+1 (with PLO event), the daemon writes version N+1' (with task completion) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d one overwrites the other, corrupting the hash chain. Phase 3 will increase state_admin write frequency because PLO phase-state tracking is now a first-class discipline, making this race more likely to materialize than it was in Phase 2.",
            "trigger_conditions": "Operator runs any state_admin write command (bank, phase implementing/demo-released/po-satisfied, retro phase-transition, promote-candidate) while the daemon process is alive and in its 2-second idle poll between task dispatches. The race window is the duration of state_admin's load\u00e2\u2020\u2019modify\u00e2\u2020\u2019save cycle (~50\u00e2\u20ac\u201c200ms on OneDrive). Given that PLO tracking commands will be issued mid-session during Phase 3, and the daemon is typically left running, concurrent access is the default operational pattern.",
            "estimated_likelihood": "medium",
            "estimated_blast_radius": "substrate_wide"
