            "evidence": [
              "fnsr_state_verification.py:491-500 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d PREDICATES loop catches all exceptions with bare `except Exception as e:` and appends a finding with `severity: 'info'`, `drift_kind: 'predicate_error'`; no operator alert or elevated severity",
              "fnsr_state_verification.py:530-535 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d `--blocking-only` filter shows only findings with severity='block'; a predicate_error at info severity is invisible to `--blocking-only` invocations",
              "fnsr_state_verification.py:513 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d `has_blocking_drift: severity_counts['block'] > 0` \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a predicate_error finding does NOT increment block count; report surface says 'no blocking drift' even when git predicates failed",
              "fnsr_state_verification.py:39-50 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d _git_run() uses 10-second timeout; raises no exception on timeout (returns returncode -1 and exception string in stderr), but other exception paths in git-dependent predicates (e.g., subprocess.TimeoutExpired if timeout parameter changes, OSError on lock contention) propagate to the outer try/except",
              "fnsr_daemon.py:118-120 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d _atomic_write docstring explicitly names OneDrive sync, antivirus, Search indexer as sources of PermissionError on the same filesystem path; the same conditions apply to git subprocess invocations from fnsr_state_verification.py"
            ],
            "rationale": "The SVG predicates svg_2_1_commit_gap, svg_3_1_push_gap, and svg_7_1_demo_released_without_deploy all invoke git subprocesses. When these subprocesses fail for environmental reasons (OneDrive sync lock contention, antivirus scanner holding git index, Search indexer interfering with .git/), the exception propagates into the outer loop's catch-all and is logged as severity='info'. The operator running `python fnsr_state_verification.py --blocking-only` sees zero blocking findings \u00e2\u20ac\u201d which is technically correct (no block-class finding was appended) but operationally misleading: the predicates that would detect a commit-gap or push-gap have silently been skipped. This is the inverse of what a gate should do: failures-to-check should escalate severity, not suppress it.",
            "trigger_conditions": "Operator runs `python fnsr_state_verification.py` (or the stall-watch's SVG integration) on Windows with OneDrive sync, Windows Defender, or Windows Search Indexer active on the GraphWrite project directory. Any git subprocess that raises an uncaught exception (subprocess.TimeoutExpired at t>10s, OSError on .git/ lock, PermissionError on .git/index) causes the corresponding predicate to produce a predicate_error finding at info severity. The report's has_blocking_drift field shows False, git predicates appear not-fired rather than failed, and the operator proceeds without knowing the drift check was skipped.",
