      "outputs": {
        "changes": [
          {
            "id": "C1",
            "file": "src/import/turtle-import.ts",
            "rationale": "Add Quad to the n3 import so the explicit Quad[] type annotation in C2 resolves. Matches the import pattern already established in src/emit/turtle.ts (`import { Writer, DataFactory, Quad } from \"n3\"`).",
            "before": "import { Parser } from \"n3\";",
            "after": "import { Parser, Quad } from \"n3\";",
            "scope": "minimal"
          },
          {
            "id": "C2",
            "file": "src/import/turtle-import.ts",
            "rationale": "Root cause of both TS errors: `ReturnType<typeof parser.parse>` resolves to `void` because TypeScript's ReturnType picks the last overload of an overloaded function \u00e2\u20ac\u201d and the last Parser.parse overload (callback form) returns void. Fixes: (1) replace `let quads: ReturnType<typeof parser.parse>` with `const quads: Quad[] = []` (now explicitly Quad[], not void \u00e2\u20ac\u201d fixes TS2322 at 143,5); (2) switch to the callback overload so parser.parse() is called for its side effect (callback fills quads[]) rather than its return value \u00e2\u20ac\u201d quads is now Quad[] which is iterable (fixes TS2488 at 159,22). The try/catch is preserved: if the callback throws err, the exception propagates synchronously out of parser.parse() because N3.js executes callbacks synchronously on string input. Error handling and contentHash logic are unchanged.",
            "before": "  // Parse with N3.js (\u00c3\u201a\u00c2\u00a715.4: normative RDF library)\n  const parser = new Parser();\n  let quads: ReturnType<typeof parser.parse>;\n  try {\n    quads = parser.parse(turtleSource);\n  } catch (e) {\n    return {\n      ok: false,\n      error: `Turtle parse error: ${e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e)}`,\n      code: \"PARSE_ERROR\",\n    };\n  }",
            "after": "  // Parse with N3.js (\u00c3\u201a\u00c2\u00a715.4: normative RDF library)\n  const parser = new Parser();\n  const quads: Quad[] = [];\n  try {\n    parser.parse(turtleSource, (err, quad) => {\n      if (err) throw err;\n      if (quad) quads.push(quad);\n    });\n  } catch (e) {\n    return {\n      ok: false,\n      error: `Turtle parse error: ${e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e)}`,\n      code: \"PARSE_ERROR\",\n    };\n  }",
            "scope": "minimal"
          }
        ],
        "tests_recommended": [
          {
            "file": "tests/import-turtle.test.ts",
            "describe": "All 6 existing AC tests (AC1\u00e2\u20ac\u201cAC6) should pass after this fix with no modification to the test file. AC5 (SIZE_EXCEEDED pre-parse rejection) and the implicit PARSE_ERROR path exercise the two error branches; AC1\u00e2\u20ac\u201cAC4 and AC6 exercise the happy path including quad collection, rdfs:Class normalization, subClassOf, and contentHash."
          }
        ],
        "open_questions": [
          "A simpler type-only fix also exists: change `let quads: ReturnType<typeof parser.parse>` to `let quads: Quad[]` and keep the synchronous `parser.parse(turtleSource)` call \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the first overload `parse(input: string, callback?: null): Q[]` returns Quad[] and the try/catch already handles synchronous throws. The task explicitly requests the callback pattern, so this proposal follows that instruction; but the type-annotation-only approach is available if the operator prefers minimum diff.",
          "ParseCallback<Q> in @types/n3 types `error` as `Error` (non-nullable) and `quad` as `Q` (non-nullable), even though N3.js passes (null, null) at end-of-parse. The `if (err)` and `if (quad)` guards are valid JS runtime checks; TypeScript does not error on truthy checks against non-nullable object types in strict mode."
        ],
        "summary": "Two minimal changes to src/import/turtle-import.ts: (C1) add Quad to the n3 import; (C2) replace the sync-return parse block with the N3.js callback-collection pattern (const quads: Quad[] = []; parser.parse(turtleSource, (err, quad) => { if (err) throw err; if (quad) quads.push(quad); })). Fixes TS2322 at line 143 and TS2488 at line 159. No other files modified. Error handling, contentHash logic, and all downstream quad-processing code are unchanged.",
        "self_assessment": "confident"
      }
    },
