        "summary": "The root cause of the 4 failing ACs (AC1, AC2, AC3, AC6) is a single architectural mismatch in src/import/turtle-import.ts lines 143\u00e2\u20ac\u201c146: the N3 Parser's `parse()` method is called with a callback, which triggers the asynchronous code path in N3Lexer.js (line 477: `queueMicrotask(() => this._tokenizeToEnd(callback, true))`). When a callback is provided with a string input, the N3 Lexer defers all tokenization to a microtask \u00e2\u20ac\u201d meaning parse() returns immediately with the quads array still empty. The downstream term-extraction loop (lines 162\u00e2\u20ac\u201c205) then iterates over zero quads and builds zero terms. The synchronous overload (`parser.parse(input)` with no callback) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d as used in src/emit/n-triples.ts line 35 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d processes all tokens before returning and directly returns the Quad array; this is the known-working pattern in the codebase. The term-extraction logic itself (termType checks, value access, IRI constant matching) is correct and would work once quads are populated. AC4 and AC5 pass because they operate on the raw source bytes before any parsed-quad data is needed.",
