            "source": "CLAUDE.md"
          }
        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "When a vendor library exposes overloaded entry points distinguished only by argument arity (here: N3 Parser.parse with vs. without a callback), the sync-vs-async dispatch branch is invisible at the TypeScript surface but decisive at runtime. The same codebase contained both the working synchronous call (n-triples.ts:35) and the broken async-callback call (turtle-import.ts:143) side by side, with neither the type system nor the tests catching the mismatch until an integration AC observed the empty quads[]. Subject-project guidance: prefer the synchronous overload when the consumer's control flow is itself synchronous, and consider a thin internal wrapper that pins the overload at the import boundary to prevent regression."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "Recon\u00e2\u2020\u2019ratification handoff worked cleanly here because recon produced excerpt-anchored findings with file:line pairs (F1\u00e2\u20ac\u201cF10), making independent verification by the architect a sub-minute spot-check rather than a re-investigation. This is the intended Spec 03 shape: the architect's ratification quality is rate-limited by how cite-checkable the recon output is, not by how voluminous it is. Recommend continuing to require excerpt+file+lines triples in recon outputs."
