      "attempts": 1,
      "outputs": {
        "diagnosis": "Applier 755-apply-p3-c1c failed with before_not_found on src\\import\\turtle-import.ts change C1. Root cause: the developer's `before` snippet (state.jsonld line 82831) includes the comment line `// Parse with N3.js (\\u00c3\\u201a\\u00c2\\u00a715.4: normative RDF library)` which decodes to `\u00c3\u0192\u00e2\u20ac\u0161\u00c3\u201a\u00c2\u00a715.4` (double mojibake). The actual disk content at turtle-import.ts:139 is `// Parse with N3.js (\u00c3\u201a\u00c2\u00a715.4: normative RDF library)` (single mojibake `\u00c3\u201a\u00c2\u00a7`, verified via Read). The dev's snippet has an extra `\u00c3\u0192\u00e2\u20ac\u0161` (U+00C3 U+201A) prefix that does not exist on disk, so the applier's strict byte-for-byte `before` match fails on the very first line. The substantive change (lines 141-153: `const`\u00e2\u2020\u2019`let` + sync overload of `parser.parse(turtleSource)` returning Quad[] directly) is pure ASCII, correctly ratified at 754-rat-p3-c1c, and untouched by this mojibake issue. Recovery: brief-confirmation chain with a re-emitted change whose `before` is anchored on the ASCII-only block starting at `const parser = new Parser();`, skipping the comment line entirely. Per CLAUDE.md \u00c2\u00a77.8 (gap-7/gap-8), `mojibake-repair` is NOT inserted \u00e2\u20ac\u201d it would clean both `before` and `after` uniformly and still mismatch the mojibake-laden disk.",
