        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "Adapter-boundary term-augmentation pattern: the kernel-side importOntology() in src/import/turtle-import.ts returns ImportedTermObject values containing only the \u00c2\u00a714.1 extraction-scope fields (id, type, rdfs:label/comment/subClassOf/subPropertyOf), deliberately omitting the \u00c2\u00a75.7 project-document-shape fields (ecm:source, ecm:ontologyId). The CLI adapter is then responsible for the shape lift \u00e2\u20ac\u201d augmenting each extracted term with `ecm:source: \"ecm:imported-ontology\"` and `ecm:ontologyId: result.ontology.id` before merging into project['ecm:terms']. This split is a deliberate separation of extraction concerns (kernel) from project-document-shape concerns (adapter), and it composes with validate()'s ecm:source-based gating of reserved-IRI checks. Worth surfacing because the gap between the two shapes is invisible at the function-signature level \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a future contributor reading only importOntology()'s return type would not see that the project-document-shape fields are owed by the caller. Candidate for an architectural note in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md or an inline JSDoc on importOntology() if the pattern recurs across other Phase 3 import paths."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "The proposal applies the CLI-boundary determinism discipline correctly: createdAt is computed once at the case-block boundary (`values.clock ?? new Date().toISOString()`), then threaded down through cmdImportOntology to importOntology(), which never recomputes. This is the canonical pattern for honoring kernel purity while permitting wall-clock at adapters, and the --clock override gives deterministic overrides for tests (used by C6). Pattern is identical to the unimplemented expectation set by the existing --clock/--seed/--deterministic flags (recon F9) which were parsed but never threaded \u00e2\u20ac\u201d this change is the first concrete consumer of values.clock, so subsequent CLI commands that need wall-clock timestamps now have a precedent to follow."
          }
        ]
      },
