            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "React hook-ordering constraints force defensive guards on hook-using function components to be placed AFTER all useState/useEffect calls, not at the top of the function body. This produces a recognizable shape: the 'earliest safe guard site' is immediately after the last hook call and immediately before any handler closures. Sub-task D's placement (after six useState calls, before handleSubmit) is a canonical instance of this pattern. Future component-level defensive guards in this codebase should expect to follow the same shape; a structural heuristic for ratification is: if a proposed guard appears BEFORE any hook call in a hook-using component, the change is broken regardless of its logical content."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "Belt-and-suspenders defensive guards layered behind a primary gate (here: TermSidebar.tsx isClickable as primary, EditTermDialog null-return as secondary) form a defense-in-depth pattern where the secondary guard's correct behavior under normal operation is to never fire. Ratification of such guards turns on (a) confirming the primary gate exists and is cited in the rationale, (b) confirming the secondary guard does not alter the unguarded path, and (c) confirming the failure mode if both gates fail is silent (null/no-op) rather than crash. The developer's rationale citing F11 (primary gate location) is the audit-surfacing mechanism for this pattern."
          }
        ]
      },
