        "purpose": "Reconnaissance for Phase 3 Chain 4. Aaron 2026-06-02 identified a missing capability: GraphWrite has a Turtle import engine (Chain 1) and a CLI import command (Chain 2), but NO browser-side affordance to upload a .ttl or .jsonld ontology file (e.g., dist-tests/BFO.jsonld) into the current project. The 'Open' button only loads GraphWrite project documents (ecm:terms etc. already populated), not raw ontology sources. This chain ships a browser Import Ontology dialog + button + browser-compat SHA-256 shim so the engine can run client-side. Investigate: (1) src/import/turtle-import.ts exact node:crypto usage and the function signature of importOntology -- identify minimal change to accept an injectable digest function while preserving Chain 1 CLI behavior; (2) src/ui/App.tsx header structure (gw-btn-new, gw-btn-open, gw-btn-save, etc.) and existing file picker pattern (gw-file-input); the new Import button should sit alongside Open; (3) src/ui/Dialog.tsx convention for new dialogs (RemapDialog from Chain 3.2-UI is the most recent reference); (4) how the currently-loaded project state flows through App.tsx -- where the merge-into-project for imported terms + ImportedOntologyRecord lands (analogous to Chain 2 CLI cmdImportOntology merge logic but client-side, against in-memory project state); (5) tests/playwright/ scaffold + a candidate small .ttl fixture (the existing examples/ directory or tests/playwright/fixtures/ may have one; if not, the chain may need to create a minimal one); (6) Web Crypto API SHA-256 invocation pattern (async crypto.subtle.digest returning ArrayBuffer; needs base64/hex conversion to match the existing 'sha256-<hex>' string format from node:crypto). Output findings shaped for FOUR downstream developer agents (one per sub-task A/B/C/D). Note: JSON-LD ontology parsing (sub-task E from the recommendation) is deferred -- Chain 4 supports .ttl only, matching Chain 1's engine. JSON-LD ontology support stays forward-tracked.",
