        "summary": "Phase 3 Chain 4 requires adding a browser-side Import Ontology affordance. The core kernel function importOntology (src/import/turtle-import.ts:121\u00e2\u20ac\u201c247) is synchronous and has three non-browser-compatible dependencies: import of node:crypto's createHash (line 23), use of Node.js Buffer.from() for UTF-8 byte encoding (line 128), and a transitive call to generateIri() which uses node:crypto.randomUUID() (line 234). N3.js (the Turtle parser) is already isomorphic. Six synchronous call sites in tests/import-turtle.test.ts and src/cli/index.ts must stay unbroken, so Sub-task A's minimal change is an injectable digest function parameter (optional 5th arg) or a parallel browser-entry-point file. The browser Web Crypto equivalents are: TextEncoder().encode() for Buffer, crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', ...) (async, returns ArrayBuffer) for SHA-256, and crypto.randomUUID() for IRI generation \u00e2\u20ac\u201d all of which are already used in four existing UI components. App.tsx header (lines 257\u00e2\u20ac\u201c309) has five buttons in gw-btn-group and one hidden file input gw-file-input accepting .jsonld/.json only; the Import Ontology button sits alongside Open (disabled when project is null), backed by a second hidden file input accepting .ttl. The Dialog primitive (Dialog.tsx) and RemapDialog (most recent addition, testId 'gw-dialog-remap') define the dialog convention for Sub-task C. No .ttl fixture exists in tests/playwright/fixtures/ \u00e2\u20ac\u201d Sub-task D must create one (e.g., tests/playwright/fixtures/minimal-ontology.ttl) following the inline SMALL_TTL strings already present in tests/import-turtle.test.ts. The CLI merge logic at src/cli/index.ts:321\u00e2\u20ac\u201c344 is the reference implementation for the browser-side project state merge.",
