        "ruling": "ratified",
        "editorial_verdict": "substantive",
        "editorial_verdict_reason": "Adds a new kernel module (src/kernel/sha256.ts), refactors the importOntology contract with new TypeScript overload signatures and an injected digest function, and ships a new test file \u00e2\u20ac\u201d these are behavioral and API-surface changes, far outside the editorial scope of typo/formatting/citation-format corrections.",
        "rationale": "Sub-task A's proposal satisfies all five verification checkpoints from the task purpose. (1) The shim cleanly exposes sha256Hex (sync, node:crypto) and sha256HexAsync (async, globalThis.crypto.subtle) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d both accept Uint8Array|string and return 64-char lowercase hex. (2) Hex format is preserved: the `sha256-${digest}` template at the new line 240-equivalent in C4 is byte-identical to the prior implementation, and AC2/AC4/AC5 in tests/sha256.test.ts cross-validate the shim outputs against the createHash reference, locking the format invariant. (3) The refactor is non-breaking by construction: three TypeScript overload signatures in C3 preserve the synchronous `ImportResult` return type for the six existing call sites in tests/import-turtle.test.ts and src/cli/index.ts (recon F4), while the new optional fifth parameter activates the async Promise<ImportResult> branch only for callers that explicitly opt in. The Buffer.from \u00e2\u2020\u2019 TextEncoder.encode swap is byte-safe: createHash.update() accepts Uint8Array, the byte count is identical to Buffer.byteLength for valid UTF-8 (recon F2), and the SIZE_EXCEEDED check at line 129 keeps producing the same numeric value. (4) No browser globals leak into the node path \u00e2\u20ac\u201d globalThis.crypto.subtle is invoked only inside the `digestHexFn !== undefined` async IIFE branch; the synchronous else-branch retains createHash exclusively. TextEncoder is a Node 11+ and browser global, so using it in both paths does not introduce a browser-only dependency on the node side. (5) Layer purity is preserved: scripts/ensure-kernel-purity.ts explicitly allows `node:` builtins in its FORBIDDEN_PATTERNS regexes (the kernel already imports node:fs/promises and node:path in src/kernel/index.ts), so node:crypto in src/kernel/sha256.ts will pass the purity gate. The developer's open question #1 (Vite cannot bundle sha256.ts as written when UI imports sha256HexAsync) is a real concern but lies in sub-task B's adjudication scope \u00e2\u20ac\u201d it does not block sub-task A's contract, which is fully testable in Node via the proposed tests/sha256.test.ts. The mojibake comment fix on line 127 (C3 before: `\u00c3\u201a\u00c2\u00a712.2` \u00e2\u2020\u2019 after: `\u00c2\u00a712.2`) is a legitimate editorial correction embedded in the substantive change; the new content in C1/C2/C3/C4 contains no non-ASCII characters that would require mojibake-repair. The proposal is ratified for Pass 2b applier dispatch.",
