      "inputs": {
        "phase": "phase-3",
        "mode": "ratification",
        "chain_label": "Phase 3 Chain 4 sub-task B -- Pass 2a ratification (ImportOntologyDialog)",
        "purpose": "Pass 2a ratification for sub-task B. Verify: (1) dialog follows RemapDialog.tsx structural pattern; (2) 50MB hard-reject runs BEFORE FileReader (cheap byte-length check); (3) merge shape matches Chain 2 CLI (same ecm:source + ecm:ontologyId augmentation); (4) .jsonld input rejection with clear message (Turtle only for v0.4); (5) test-ids match the gw-* convention used elsewhere.",
        "spec_refs": [
          "FNSR Spec 03",
          "SPEC section 12.2",
          "IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN section 3.1"
        ]
      },
      "attempts": 1,
      "outputs": {
        "ruling": "denied",
        "editorial_verdict": "substantive",
        "editorial_verdict_reason": "Creates a new UI dialog component implementing a previously CLI-only feature (browser-side Turtle ontology import) with a new state-machine, async I/O surface, project-state mutation path, and five new test-ids; this is substantive feature work, not typo/formatting/terminology tightening.",
        "rationale": "Reconnaissance is present in UPSTREAM (urn:fnsr:task:808-recon-p3-c4), so the refusal contract is satisfied; ratification proceeds on substance. Two criteria fail.\n\n(1) Verification criterion #2 NOT MET: the task explicitly required '50MB hard-reject runs BEFORE FileReader (cheap byte-length check)'. The proposed handleFileChange goes .jsonld-guard -> setPhase('parsing') -> reader.readAsText(file) with no inspection of file.size. The 50MB ceiling is delegated to importOntology's SIZE_EXCEEDED path, which fires only AFTER FileReader has fully loaded the file into memory as a JavaScript string. A multi-GB file picked accidentally or maliciously would be loaded into the renderer before being rejected, defeating the architectural intent of the cheap pre-check. The developer's rationale ('50 MB hard-reject is delegated to importOntology's SIZE_EXCEEDED path') confirms the miss is intentional and not an oversight in shape; it is an interpretation that contradicts the stated criterion. Remediation is a ~5-line guard between the .jsonld branch and reader.readAsText(file): if (file.size > 50 * 1024 * 1024) { setPhase('error'); setErrorMessage(...); return; }.\n\n(2) Developer-flagged BLOCKING runtime failure (self_assessment: 'uncertain'): importOntology's async branch calls generateIri('ecm:uuid-urn', {}) at turtle-import.ts:254, and generateIri imports nodeRandomUUID from node:crypto at the module top-level in src/iri/index.ts:20 (verified directly). Vite externalises node:crypto for browser builds, so the dialog will throw at runtime when an operator confirms an import. Sub-task A apparently fixed the createHash/Buffer paths via TextEncoder + injectable digestHexFn but did not extend the same injection pattern to UUID generation. This means the dialog as-ratified would be non-functional end-to-end even after applier lands it; the feature surface would compile and mount but crash on every successful parse. Structurally this is outside sub-task B's single-file scope but it is a prerequisite for the dialog being useful.\n\nOther criteria pass: the dialog follows the RemapDialog pattern (Dialog wrapper, no <form>, Cancel/Apply flex div); the merge shape mirrors CLI cmdImportOntology lines 321-344 exactly (ecm:source: 'ecm:imported-ontology' + ecm:ontologyId augmentation, spread into existing ecm:ontologies/ecm:terms); .jsonld/.json files are rejected with a clear v0.4-Turtle-only message before parsing; the five test-ids (gw-dialog-import-ontology, gw-import-file-input, gw-import-preview-count, gw-btn-import-import-cancel, gw-btn-import-confirm) follow the gw-* convention.\n\nRecommended remediation (each with a tradeoff):\n  R1. Add the file.size pre-check in handleFileChange before reader.readAsText (small, mechanical; the only blocker to ratifying sub-task B in isolation).\n  R2. Queue a separate task extending generateIri with an optional uuidFn injectable mirroring the existing digestHexFn pattern, and thread () => crypto.randomUUID() through the dialog's async path. Alternative: add a Vite resolve.alias shim for node:crypto in vite.config.ts (faster to land; broader blast radius if other call sites assume node-only). The choice is operator-territory; either should NOT be folded into sub-task B's single-file scope.\n\nThis is denial, not deferral: UPSTREAM provides sufficient evidence to rule, and the failure modes are concrete and remediable.",
        "referenced_evidence": [
          {
            "type": "upstream_task",
            "id": "urn:fnsr:task:808-recon-p3-c4",
            "field": "outputs.findings"
          },
          {
            "type": "upstream_task",
            "id": "urn:fnsr:task:812-dev-p3-c4-B",
            "field": "outputs.changes"
          },
          {
            "type": "upstream_task",
            "id": "urn:fnsr:task:812-dev-p3-c4-B",
            "field": "outputs.open_questions"
          },
          {
            "type": "spec_section",
            "id": "section 12.2",
            "source": "project/SPEC.md"
          },
          {
            "type": "spec_section",
