          }
        ],
        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "When a verification criterion enumerates an explicit ordering ('X runs BEFORE Y, cheap byte-length check'), 'delegate X to the downstream kernel function that also enforces X' is a tempting shortcut that satisfies the constraint semantically but misses the architectural intent: the cheap pre-check exists specifically to short-circuit the expensive downstream load. Future task authors should consider naming the architectural intent ('avoid loading rejected payloads into memory') alongside the structural rule, and developer agents should treat ordering criteria as load-bearing rather than as transferable obligations."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "discipline-correction",
            "content": "Sub-task A's browser-compat scope addressed two of three node:crypto dependencies in the import pipeline (createHash via injectable digestHexFn; Buffer.from via TextEncoder) but missed the third (nodeRandomUUID via generateIri). The injectable-dependency pattern that worked for digestHexFn was not extended to uuidFn, leaving a transitive break. Pattern: when a recon finding enumerates N non-browser-compatible call sites (recon F1 named THREE: createHash, Buffer.from, generateIri), the browser-compat sub-task's acceptance criteria should require coverage of all N sites or explicit deferral of any uncovered site; otherwise the chain ratifies a downstream-consumer (the dialog) that depends on incomplete upstream work."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "The developer agent self-assessed 'uncertain' and labeled the node:crypto/generateIri issue as BLOCKING in its open_questions output rather than silently shipping a component that compiles and mounts but crashes on use. This is the uncertainty-disclosure discipline working as designed at the developer-agent boundary, and it gave the architect the evidence to deny in Pass 2a rather than letting the breakage surface at test-runner or, worse, at operator demo. Worth preserving as a positive example of developer self_assessment doing structural work."
          }
        ]
      },
