        "editorial_verdict_reason": "Adds a new optional parameter to two public functions (generateIri, importOntology) and modifies kernel-layer code path semantics; this is behavioral surface change, not terminology tightening.",
        "rationale": "The proposed change satisfies all six verification dimensions named in the task purpose. (1) Pattern mirror: C1/C2/C3 add `uuidFn?: () => string` exactly parallel to `digestHexFn` from sub-task A \u00e2\u20ac\u201d same optional-parameter shape, same async-branch threading, same kernel-injection discipline. (2) Non-breaking: the parameter is optional at every level (generateIri sig, importOntology sync overload, async overload); all six sync call sites enumerated in recon F4 (cli/index.ts:306; five tests/import-turtle.test.ts sites) continue to dispatch the no-arg path that falls through to generateUuidV4() / node:crypto. (3) Call-site threading: C3 patches the single generateIri invocation inside importOntology's async branch (turtle-import.ts:234 per recon F1), passing uuidFn through; no other call sites in src/import/ require updates because the sync branch retains the node fallback. (4) No Web Crypto leak into node fallback: the kernel-side fallback path `uuidFn !== undefined ? uuidFn() : generateUuidV4()` keeps Web Crypto exclusively inside the injected closure; generateUuidV4() retains its node:crypto.randomUUID() body unchanged. (5) Layer purity: src/iri/ stays a kernel module; the injection inverts dependency direction so browser-environment knowledge lives at the call site (App.tsx / Dialog), not in the kernel \u00e2\u20ac\u201d this is the orthodox kernel-injection pattern per CLAUDE.md \u00c2\u00a79. (6) Test coverage: C4's four ACs cover injected-verbatim (AC1), node-fallback non-breaking (AC2), call-count invariant (AC3 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d guards against accidental double-invocation), and ecm:deterministic isolation (AC4 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d guards against the uuidFn leaking into the UUIDv5 path). Reconnaissance evidence (urn:fnsr:task:808-recon-p3-c4) is present in UPSTREAM and substantively justifies the change (F1 identifies the generateIri \u00e2\u2020\u2019 node:crypto.randomUUID transitive call as one of the three browser-incompat sites; F6 confirms crypto.randomUUID() is browser-available; F13 confirms tsconfig.ui.json excludes node types, so the kernel injection contract is the right shape). The developer's open question Q2 (module-level static `import { ... randomUUID as nodeRandomUUID } from 'node:crypto'` may still cause Vite bundle-time failure even with runtime injection) is a real residual risk and is correctly flagged as a possible follow-up structural task rather than a blocker for this sub-task \u00e2\u20ac\u201d establishing the injection contract is independently valuable, and the bundling question is properly scoped outside A2.",
