        "bankings": [
          {
            "id": "B1",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "Signal-vs-act separation in multi-sub-task chains: sub-task A widens the type union and computes the threshold signal, but does NOT perform the state mutation that the union enables (ecm:importStatus='ecm:degraded' assignment lives in sub-task B's dialog flow). This is a clean instance of the producer-signals-consumer-acts pattern across a layer boundary (importer \u00e2\u2020\u2019 dialog) and across sub-task boundaries within a chain. Future chains decomposing a feature into A/B/C/D sub-tasks should preserve this discipline \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the type-layer change can land before the behavior-layer change because the union value is forward-compatible (the hardcoded 'ecm:parsed' assignment still type-checks under the widened union)."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "methodology-refinement-candidate",
            "content": "Code-surface vs. SPEC-text amendment ordering within a chain: the C2 type widening introduces 'ecm:degraded' as a permitted ecm:importStatus value at the TypeScript level, but SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.6 schema example currently shows only 'ecm:parsed' (recon F18). The developer's open question Q1 correctly identifies that the SPEC amendment must land before or alongside sub-task B (where 'ecm:degraded' is first written) \u00e2\u20ac\u201d not before sub-task A. This is a sequencing pattern worth formalizing: type-union widening in the code surface is forward-compatible and can land independently; the SPEC amendment is gated by the first ASSIGNMENT site of the new value, not the first DECLARATION site. The architect's ratification for sub-task B should refuse if no SPEC \u00c2\u00a75.6 amendment is queued upstream or alongside."
          },
