            "content": "When the deterministic verification-ritual returns overall_status: needs_llm_judgment, the operator MUST queue verification-ritual-llm (in the mode named by evidence.llm_mode on the deferred category) upstream of the ratification task, not in parallel with it. The CLAUDE.md section 7.11 architect refusal contract refuses ratification on needs_llm_judgment without the LLM-side chain in UPSTREAM, so queuing ratification immediately after the deterministic step guarantees a denied ruling and an extra cycle. This is a chain-composition discipline; a v3.2 candidate substrate refinement is a chain-validator predicate that detects 'verification-ritual upstream of ratification with Cat 9 candidacy declared but no verification-ritual-llm sibling' and either warns at append-tasks time or auto-inserts the LLM task."
          },
          {
            "id": "B2",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "An editorial documentation-alignment amendment to a SPEC subsection that contains no Q-rulings, reason-codes, FOL/OWL @type discriminators, manifest mirrors, cross-phase cycle artifacts, or IRI registries produces a verification-ritual result where 7 of 10 categories return miss/missing_canonical_source vacuously (the artifact has no surface on which those categories can adjudicate). This is structurally distinct from miss/missing_canonical_source where the artifact DOES cite those surfaces but the canonical sources are absent from inputs \u00e2\u20ac\u201d yet both cases share one miss_class. A future verification-ritual refinement could distinguish category-not-applicable-to-artifact (no veto risk; pass-equivalent) from canonical-source-absent (operator must supply), reducing operator cognitive overhead when reading per_category_result on editorial-scope amendments."
          },
          {
            "id": "B3",
            "category": "pattern-observation",
            "content": "Cat 1 (Spec-Section-Existence) returned pass with note 'no section N.M citations in artifact' even though the amendment text contains the strings 'section 14.2' and 'IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN section 3.3 AC4'. The Cat 1 predicate appears to scan for a typographic section-marker glyph rather than the literal token 'section N.M'. This is not a defect for the current artifact (the references are real and recon-validated) but is a citation-format-coverage observation: future deliberation may decide whether the Cat 1 predicate should also recognize plain 'section N.M' references, or whether SPEC editorial discipline should enforce the section-marker form."
          }
        ]
      },
